WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.275
&gt;&gt; Now, I want to introduce

00:00:16.276 --> 00:00:18.645
our first keynote speaker

00:00:18.646 --> 00:00:22.145
who essentially is
the mastermind of Azure.

00:00:22.146 --> 00:00:28.220
Azure is the way we draw
boxes around of these things.

00:00:28.221 --> 00:00:31.250
So, Azure is of course
our public cloud,

00:00:31.251 --> 00:00:35.030
but Azure or Azure Stack is also

00:00:35.031 --> 00:00:37.340
our private cloud and the two

00:00:37.341 --> 00:00:40.485
together form the hybrid cloud,

00:00:40.486 --> 00:00:43.270
and Azure results of our IoT

00:00:43.271 --> 00:00:46.655
or Edge story with
the smaller devices.

00:00:46.656 --> 00:00:51.890
So, the mastermind behind
that is Mark Russinovich,

00:00:51.891 --> 00:00:55.190
who is the CTO of Azure.

00:00:55.191 --> 00:00:59.745
He got his PhD from
Carnegie Mellon University,

00:00:59.746 --> 00:01:01.640
and since then, has had

00:01:01.641 --> 00:01:05.685
a very fruitful career with
many, many activities.

00:01:05.686 --> 00:01:10.070
So, he's really passionate
about distributed systems,

00:01:10.071 --> 00:01:14.790
about security, about
operating systems.

00:01:14.791 --> 00:01:19.105
He's been working on
Windows and Azure

00:01:19.106 --> 00:01:24.430
obviously for a long time and
he has created many tools,

00:01:24.431 --> 00:01:27.020
so he's the author of the system,

00:01:27.021 --> 00:01:30.520
integration, administration
tools for Windows.

00:01:30.521 --> 00:01:35.820
He had several stints
among others at IBM.

00:01:35.821 --> 00:01:41.980
But in 1996, he started
a company called Winternals,

00:01:41.981 --> 00:01:46.970
which was eventually acquired
by Microsoft in 2006.

00:01:46.971 --> 00:01:50.885
Since 2006, he has been
with Microsoft and

00:01:50.886 --> 00:01:55.205
has essentially help create

00:01:55.206 --> 00:01:58.305
Azure and holding Azure together.

00:01:58.306 --> 00:02:02.210
One of the very interesting
facts about Mark is that

00:02:02.211 --> 00:02:03.950
he's a very passionate speaker

00:02:03.951 --> 00:02:06.040
but also a very
passionate author,

00:02:06.041 --> 00:02:07.610
and he wrote books,

00:02:07.611 --> 00:02:09.115
not only technical books,

00:02:09.116 --> 00:02:11.270
but also fictional books and

00:02:11.271 --> 00:02:15.550
fictional technique
called fictional books.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.610
I haven't read them,

00:02:17.611 --> 00:02:21.255
but for now I have three books,

00:02:21.256 --> 00:02:23.960
but I've actually looked at
it and I'm going to read

00:02:23.961 --> 00:02:26.675
one of them and you
can look them up.

00:02:26.676 --> 00:02:28.330
So with that, please welcome

00:02:28.331 --> 00:02:31.120
Mark Russinovich.
Thank you very much.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.270
&gt;&gt; So, I'm really

00:02:35.271 --> 00:02:37.845
disappointed to hear
he hasn't read them.

00:02:37.846 --> 00:02:41.280
Well, anyway, thank
you for having me.

00:02:41.281 --> 00:02:42.380
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:02:42.381 --> 00:02:44.270
I want to thank I'm NSR
for inviting me to kick

00:02:44.271 --> 00:02:46.885
this off Faculty Research Summit.

00:02:46.886 --> 00:02:49.400
I'm the Chief Technology
Officer of Azure.

00:02:49.401 --> 00:02:51.695
I've been in Azure for
eight years, and in that role,

00:02:51.696 --> 00:02:52.970
I work on the
technical strategy and

00:02:52.971 --> 00:02:54.325
architecture for the platform.

00:02:54.326 --> 00:02:57.260
Now, this morning I've got
about an hour with you

00:02:57.261 --> 00:03:00.600
and the topic of this session
is Azure Architecture.

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:04.640
Azure architecture has grown
over the last 10 years since

00:03:04.641 --> 00:03:06.620
Azure was really
started to become

00:03:06.621 --> 00:03:08.750
a very broad and
complicated platform.

00:03:08.751 --> 00:03:10.370
So, 60 minutes is certainly not

00:03:10.371 --> 00:03:12.410
enough time to do it justice.

00:03:12.411 --> 00:03:14.000
I can give you
a superficial look at

00:03:14.001 --> 00:03:16.435
the architecture
broad brush strokes,

00:03:16.436 --> 00:03:18.080
but I thought it in the process,

00:03:18.081 --> 00:03:20.420
I'd also share with you insights

00:03:20.421 --> 00:03:21.890
into some of the
collaboration that

00:03:21.891 --> 00:03:23.390
we've had going with
Microsoft Research.

00:03:23.391 --> 00:03:25.550
I think Microsoft
Research in my view

00:03:25.551 --> 00:03:27.930
is competitive advantage
we have here at Microsoft.

00:03:27.931 --> 00:03:31.420
Having this research body where
people are off exploring,

00:03:31.421 --> 00:03:32.660
sometimes what we think are

00:03:32.661 --> 00:03:35.775
crazy ideas but then
proved to be interesting.

00:03:35.776 --> 00:03:38.340
We actually have incorporated
many of these ideas.

00:03:38.341 --> 00:03:39.650
We partnered very
deeply with them

00:03:39.651 --> 00:03:42.010
across a number of
different projects.

00:03:42.011 --> 00:03:43.970
So, during this
architecture overview,

00:03:43.971 --> 00:03:46.100
I'm going to weave in
some of those projects

00:03:46.101 --> 00:03:49.070
and show how they've
impacted Azure.

00:03:49.071 --> 00:03:51.330
So, Microsoft Data Centers.

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:52.610
Let me talk a little bit about

00:03:52.611 --> 00:03:55.175
the overall data center strategy,

00:03:55.176 --> 00:03:58.130
which is to divide
the world into geographies.

00:03:58.131 --> 00:04:00.190
We divide the world
into geographies

00:04:00.191 --> 00:04:03.840
largely based on data
sovereignty boundaries.

00:04:03.841 --> 00:04:07.325
More and more we see
customers wanting

00:04:07.326 --> 00:04:09.050
Azure Data Centers within

00:04:09.051 --> 00:04:12.170
their country because of
the regulatory requirements,

00:04:12.171 --> 00:04:14.420
data sovereignty requirements of

00:04:14.421 --> 00:04:16.040
businesses to have the data

00:04:16.041 --> 00:04:17.935
within the country boundaries.

00:04:17.936 --> 00:04:19.550
So, this is why we're now at

00:04:19.551 --> 00:04:22.550
54 regions worldwide across

00:04:22.551 --> 00:04:24.140
a number of
different geographies,

00:04:24.141 --> 00:04:26.400
and when we go into one
of these geographies,

00:04:26.401 --> 00:04:29.260
we go in with
at least two regions.

00:04:29.261 --> 00:04:32.540
These regions are defined

00:04:32.541 --> 00:04:35.370
by latency envelope of
less than two milliseconds.

00:04:35.371 --> 00:04:37.060
So, going from any data center to

00:04:37.061 --> 00:04:39.485
any other data center within
what we call a region,

00:04:39.486 --> 00:04:40.985
what we expose to customers,

00:04:40.986 --> 00:04:42.965
is less than two milliseconds.

00:04:42.966 --> 00:04:45.605
The region pairs, we call them,

00:04:45.606 --> 00:04:47.720
are two regions that are

00:04:47.721 --> 00:04:49.910
designed for customers
to be able to build

00:04:49.911 --> 00:04:52.160
a disaster recovery solution

00:04:52.161 --> 00:04:54.660
on top of any of our
services that support

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:57.170
geo data replication are required

00:04:57.171 --> 00:04:59.630
to support replication
between the geo pairs,

00:04:59.631 --> 00:05:02.315
although they can replicate
to other regions as well.

00:05:02.316 --> 00:05:06.290
The distance between them is
typically several 100 miles

00:05:06.291 --> 00:05:08.180
because we want to place
them far enough apart

00:05:08.181 --> 00:05:10.125
to survive large-scale disasters.

00:05:10.126 --> 00:05:14.635
So, large hurricanes,
large earthquakes, floods.

00:05:14.636 --> 00:05:16.595
But within the region,

00:05:16.596 --> 00:05:20.930
we actually divide the region
into availability zones.

00:05:20.931 --> 00:05:24.590
Availability zones give
customers the ability to create

00:05:24.591 --> 00:05:26.930
a highly available solution on

00:05:26.931 --> 00:05:29.895
top of data centers
that can fail.

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:32.730
So, data centers we see
can have power outages,

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:34.615
they can have
local floods and fires,

00:05:34.616 --> 00:05:36.365
they can also be impacted by

00:05:36.366 --> 00:05:39.415
other environmental
conditions in the area.

00:05:39.416 --> 00:05:42.560
So, we typically place the
data centers that make up

00:05:42.561 --> 00:05:44.400
these availability zones several

00:05:44.401 --> 00:05:46.140
kilometers apart at least.

00:05:46.141 --> 00:05:49.640
The requirement there
again is that they've got

00:05:49.641 --> 00:05:51.050
the latency envelope between

00:05:51.051 --> 00:05:53.420
the data centers
that the region has,

00:05:53.421 --> 00:05:56.300
and less than two milliseconds
enables customers to

00:05:56.301 --> 00:05:58.250
build a solution that

00:05:58.251 --> 00:06:00.850
synchronously replicates data
across the data centers.

00:06:00.851 --> 00:06:03.095
Such that, a service that

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:05.705
uses quorum to
commit durable data,

00:06:05.706 --> 00:06:08.630
like our storage services
or our database service,

00:06:08.631 --> 00:06:11.540
can replicate the data across
three availability zones,

00:06:11.541 --> 00:06:13.490
and that's our minimum
that we go into a region

00:06:13.491 --> 00:06:16.105
with when we light up
with availability zones.

00:06:16.106 --> 00:06:17.795
Then, they can tolerate

00:06:17.796 --> 00:06:19.820
the loss of a full
availability zone or

00:06:19.821 --> 00:06:21.770
the data centers
associated with it

00:06:21.771 --> 00:06:24.060
and still continue to
serve right operations.

00:06:24.061 --> 00:06:26.240
They can actually tolerate
too and still continue

00:06:26.241 --> 00:06:29.175
to serve read data
off of their service.

00:06:29.176 --> 00:06:32.775
Now, this is
the high-level strategy.

00:06:32.776 --> 00:06:35.080
I thought it's always
fun to take a look at

00:06:35.081 --> 00:06:37.540
actual pictures of data centers,

00:06:37.541 --> 00:06:39.385
and so I've got some
to share with you

00:06:39.386 --> 00:06:41.790
just to show you the kind of
scale that we're getting to.

00:06:41.791 --> 00:06:45.340
The amount of capital investment
now Microsoft is putting

00:06:45.341 --> 00:06:47.260
into our data center build out

00:06:47.261 --> 00:06:49.260
around the world is on
the order of $10 billion,

00:06:49.261 --> 00:06:51.205
and this is likely to grow.

00:06:51.206 --> 00:06:54.820
Microsoft Azure has doubled
over the last few years,

00:06:54.821 --> 00:06:56.500
and this is a statement
that we make

00:06:56.501 --> 00:06:58.280
publicly about the
compute usage doubling.

00:06:58.281 --> 00:06:59.360
You can talk if you can see

00:06:59.361 --> 00:07:01.880
just our later latest
quarterly earnings call.

00:07:01.881 --> 00:07:04.920
We announced 89 percent
year-over-year growth

00:07:04.921 --> 00:07:06.280
of Azure revenue,

00:07:06.281 --> 00:07:08.140
and that corresponds to the build

00:07:08.141 --> 00:07:10.480
out that we've got going
with these data centers.

00:07:10.481 --> 00:07:12.570
This one is in
Quincy, Washington.

00:07:12.571 --> 00:07:13.940
Here's another look at that

00:07:13.941 --> 00:07:15.290
from a different angle
where you can see

00:07:15.291 --> 00:07:18.260
the eight data centers
that I just showed you in

00:07:18.261 --> 00:07:21.350
the picture there towards
their receding horizon,

00:07:21.351 --> 00:07:22.610
and then you can see
another data center

00:07:22.611 --> 00:07:23.900
here in the foreground.

00:07:23.901 --> 00:07:26.000
Quincy is a great place
because it's

00:07:26.001 --> 00:07:28.550
served by renewable energy
from the Columbia River,

00:07:28.551 --> 00:07:31.110
and there's plenty of
land of course there.

00:07:31.111 --> 00:07:33.750
We actually offer
data center tours there.

00:07:33.751 --> 00:07:35.575
So, sometime if you
come to campus,

00:07:35.576 --> 00:07:37.250
it's about a three-hour
drive out there,

00:07:37.251 --> 00:07:38.720
but then you can go and take

00:07:38.721 --> 00:07:40.980
a look and walk through
one of our data centers.

00:07:40.981 --> 00:07:43.260
Here's Cheyenne, Wyoming.

00:07:43.261 --> 00:07:45.095
This is another area where

00:07:45.096 --> 00:07:47.220
we've got plenty of land, energy,

00:07:47.221 --> 00:07:49.610
water to expand, and you
can see that we've got

00:07:49.611 --> 00:07:52.040
massive expansion
going on in that area.

00:07:52.041 --> 00:07:54.905
A land being cleared for
additional data center there.

00:07:54.906 --> 00:07:57.125
This is Dublin, Ireland.

00:07:57.126 --> 00:08:00.195
Another place where we've
got massive data centers.

00:08:00.196 --> 00:08:02.550
There's actually six
in this picture.

00:08:02.551 --> 00:08:04.940
There's three there on
the left and three there on

00:08:04.941 --> 00:08:08.240
the right building out over time,

00:08:08.241 --> 00:08:10.105
and we'll continue to build
new ones there as well.

00:08:10.106 --> 00:08:11.720
Amsterdam was another site.

00:08:11.721 --> 00:08:14.570
Amsterdam is actually has
plenty of great water,

00:08:14.571 --> 00:08:18.125
very reliable energy,
and you can see here,

00:08:18.126 --> 00:08:19.980
we've cleared land
for data center.

00:08:19.981 --> 00:08:21.080
There's a data center
in front of us.

00:08:21.081 --> 00:08:22.690
That building off to the left

00:08:22.691 --> 00:08:24.120
looks like it might
be date center,

00:08:24.121 --> 00:08:25.845
it's actually not a data center.

00:08:25.846 --> 00:08:27.830
Anybody know what that is?

00:08:27.831 --> 00:08:30.250
It's a greenhouse, and that means

00:08:30.251 --> 00:08:32.545
it can be growing
one of two crops,

00:08:32.546 --> 00:08:35.710
both of them legal in
the state of Washington.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:39.910
Then, here's another look
at Amsterdam,

00:08:39.911 --> 00:08:42.010
that building that we
were looking at is off

00:08:42.011 --> 00:08:44.590
there on the bottom right.

00:08:44.591 --> 00:08:46.105
So, you can see that

00:08:46.106 --> 00:08:48.920
this is taken slightly after
that previous picture,

00:08:48.921 --> 00:08:50.890
so those data centers had
been built out and there's

00:08:50.891 --> 00:08:53.745
more land being cleared
for new data centers.

00:08:53.746 --> 00:08:55.690
Now, one of the
projects that we're

00:08:55.691 --> 00:08:57.420
looking at with
Microsoft Research,

00:08:57.421 --> 00:08:59.835
and this is one Don
was talking about,

00:08:59.836 --> 00:09:02.160
Microsoft Research's goal
is to surprise us,

00:09:02.161 --> 00:09:05.595
and this one surprised
me when a few years ago,

00:09:05.596 --> 00:09:09.040
I heard about this
Project Natick.

00:09:09.041 --> 00:09:12.460
Project Natick was MSR
looking and saying,

00:09:12.461 --> 00:09:16.685
"Hey, can we go build data
centers on the ocean floor?"

00:09:16.686 --> 00:09:18.845
That just sounded crazy to me.

00:09:18.846 --> 00:09:24.330
The idea here is we can drop
tanks onto the ocean floor.

00:09:24.331 --> 00:09:27.635
We can cool them
with the the ambient

00:09:27.636 --> 00:09:30.440
water cooling that comes

00:09:30.441 --> 00:09:33.090
with being on the ocean floor
and the currents.

00:09:33.091 --> 00:09:36.005
Actually, the cooling is

00:09:36.006 --> 00:09:39.530
very efficient because it's
already cold down there.

00:09:39.531 --> 00:09:41.850
When you submerge them,

00:09:41.851 --> 00:09:44.240
the other idea is that you

00:09:44.241 --> 00:09:47.420
can then have them operate
for longer periods of time,

00:09:47.421 --> 00:09:49.190
you can expand them very
easily because you just

00:09:49.191 --> 00:09:51.595
drop additional tanks
in the water.

00:09:51.596 --> 00:09:54.155
So, the first Natick project

00:09:54.156 --> 00:09:56.570
was announced publicly
a few years ago.

00:09:56.571 --> 00:09:57.500
There was an article actually in

00:09:57.501 --> 00:09:58.700
the New York Times about it,

00:09:58.701 --> 00:10:00.830
where we dropped just
a single rack in

00:10:00.831 --> 00:10:03.800
a small cylinder into
about 30 feet of water,

00:10:03.801 --> 00:10:05.355
and we let it sit
there for a month,

00:10:05.356 --> 00:10:06.715
and then they pull it up,

00:10:06.716 --> 00:10:08.540
and it seemed like things
were still working.

00:10:08.541 --> 00:10:11.235
So, we said "Cool",
onto the next phase.

00:10:11.236 --> 00:10:13.100
This next phase is
one that we just

00:10:13.101 --> 00:10:15.680
announced about a month ago.

00:10:15.681 --> 00:10:17.915
Look at the cylinder here,

00:10:17.916 --> 00:10:20.290
it's about a little over
12 meters in length,

00:10:20.291 --> 00:10:21.800
about three meters in diameter,

00:10:21.801 --> 00:10:26.455
it has 12 racks that
are 42U height in them,

00:10:26.456 --> 00:10:29.200
and in this build out,

00:10:29.201 --> 00:10:30.510
we've got about a
thousand servers

00:10:30.511 --> 00:10:31.860
there, decommissioned
Azure servers.

00:10:31.861 --> 00:10:33.690
Of course, the storage has been

00:10:33.691 --> 00:10:34.550
pulled out and shredded

00:10:34.551 --> 00:10:35.590
on the way out of
the data center,

00:10:35.591 --> 00:10:38.280
and so there's new
hard disks and SSDs here,

00:10:38.281 --> 00:10:40.340
but they are
the actual same servers

00:10:40.341 --> 00:10:42.460
that we've been operating
in our Azure Data Centers.

00:10:42.461 --> 00:10:46.480
The tubes are built

00:10:46.481 --> 00:10:49.520
actually at a facility in
France that builds submarines,

00:10:49.521 --> 00:10:51.020
and they're very good at

00:10:51.021 --> 00:10:52.955
building these underwater
technologies.

00:10:52.956 --> 00:10:55.190
It takes 30 days.

00:10:55.191 --> 00:10:57.560
It took 30 days from
the finalization

00:10:57.561 --> 00:10:58.870
of construction at the plant in

00:10:58.871 --> 00:11:00.740
France to this being

00:11:00.741 --> 00:11:03.020
online at the bottom
of the ocean floor.

00:11:03.021 --> 00:11:04.850
So, that shows you the kind

00:11:04.851 --> 00:11:06.820
of agility that we
might get out of this.

00:11:06.821 --> 00:11:08.630
Expanding capacity
when you're doubling

00:11:08.631 --> 00:11:10.810
year-over-year is
a tough problem.

00:11:10.811 --> 00:11:11.830
If we can just simply

00:11:11.831 --> 00:11:14.465
expand on-demand
wherever we need to by

00:11:14.466 --> 00:11:16.490
simply shipping new tank
out and having it

00:11:16.491 --> 00:11:18.820
deployed within 30 days,

00:11:18.821 --> 00:11:22.325
that will let us meet
that demand very efficiently.

00:11:22.326 --> 00:11:27.350
This particular experiment has

00:11:27.351 --> 00:11:31.400
been done off the Orkney Islands
in Northern Scotland.

00:11:31.401 --> 00:11:35.150
The tank has been shipped
out to about a kilometer

00:11:35.151 --> 00:11:38.900
offshore and then submerged
into about 100 feet,

00:11:38.901 --> 00:11:41.260
36 meters of water,

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.180
and then you can
see some pictures

00:11:44.181 --> 00:11:46.725
here of it being dropped.

00:11:46.726 --> 00:11:50.520
Now, one of the benefits of this.

00:11:50.521 --> 00:11:52.380
So, what kind of constraints and

00:11:52.381 --> 00:11:54.690
interesting problems that
we're looking at here,

00:11:54.691 --> 00:11:56.770
as part of the research is

00:11:56.771 --> 00:11:59.495
when the racks are
put in that tube,

00:11:59.496 --> 00:12:01.170
all of the air is
sucked out of it,

00:12:01.171 --> 00:12:03.410
and all of the vapors
sucked out of as well.

00:12:03.411 --> 00:12:04.800
In both of these contribute

00:12:04.801 --> 00:12:07.000
to corrosive effects
on the servers.

00:12:07.001 --> 00:12:09.425
Further, once
the tube is dropped,

00:12:09.426 --> 00:12:12.150
it is in a fail in place mode,

00:12:12.151 --> 00:12:14.965
which means that as the
servers and components fail,

00:12:14.966 --> 00:12:17.850
we simply let the system degrade.

00:12:17.851 --> 00:12:20.215
Once it's become degraded enough,

00:12:20.216 --> 00:12:24.075
what we do is bring another
tube, another replacement,

00:12:24.076 --> 00:12:26.610
drop it in the water,
and then decommission,

00:12:26.611 --> 00:12:29.070
migrate workloads and data
off of the one that's

00:12:29.071 --> 00:12:32.140
being degraded onto
the new capacity.

00:12:32.141 --> 00:12:37.120
So, very efficient way to
manage the servers inside.

00:12:37.121 --> 00:12:38.815
The idea being that,

00:12:38.816 --> 00:12:40.215
the servers will last longer,

00:12:40.216 --> 00:12:42.070
so our average server lifetime in

00:12:42.071 --> 00:12:44.695
a data center is
roughly four to five years.

00:12:44.696 --> 00:12:46.310
So, we think this can extend

00:12:46.311 --> 00:12:48.265
the life cycle of those servers

00:12:48.266 --> 00:12:50.585
because in addition to

00:12:50.586 --> 00:12:53.750
the lack of corrosive effects
on the servers,

00:12:53.751 --> 00:12:55.525
there's no maintenance going on,

00:12:55.526 --> 00:12:58.300
and maintenance
disturbs the servers,

00:12:58.301 --> 00:13:00.565
which can cause problems as well.

00:13:00.566 --> 00:13:03.515
The peewee that we
get out of this,

00:13:03.516 --> 00:13:05.900
so if you take a look at
the typical advanced state of

00:13:05.901 --> 00:13:07.620
the art data centers on land

00:13:07.621 --> 00:13:09.610
that are designed with
traditional technologies,

00:13:09.611 --> 00:13:12.495
the peewees are up in
the 1.1 plus range.

00:13:12.496 --> 00:13:16.770
The peewee that we expect
on this is about 1.03.

00:13:16.771 --> 00:13:19.335
So, this offers, like I said,

00:13:19.336 --> 00:13:21.180
a bunch of potential advantages

00:13:21.181 --> 00:13:24.410
including the energy efficiency
that we get out of this.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:28.445
Let me take a look at
Azure networking now.

00:13:28.446 --> 00:13:31.160
So, if you take a look
at Azure networking,

00:13:31.161 --> 00:13:33.440
it's actually a very broad set

00:13:33.441 --> 00:13:34.750
of services and technologies.

00:13:34.751 --> 00:13:35.950
You can see some of them listed

00:13:35.951 --> 00:13:37.930
here by different categories.

00:13:37.931 --> 00:13:41.150
For example, that over in
the DC hardware space,

00:13:41.151 --> 00:13:43.105
we've got specialized
network hardware,

00:13:43.106 --> 00:13:45.090
including open-source software
that we're putting in

00:13:45.091 --> 00:13:46.260
our switches that we released to

00:13:46.261 --> 00:13:47.740
the Open Compute Project.

00:13:47.741 --> 00:13:49.070
You can see we've got a bunch of

00:13:49.071 --> 00:13:51.090
different services that are
available to customers.

00:13:51.091 --> 00:13:53.400
The core one, is
virtual networks.

00:13:53.401 --> 00:13:55.640
Virtual networks are
what the sought,

00:13:55.641 --> 00:13:58.050
the abstractions that customers

00:13:58.051 --> 00:14:00.080
bring and layer
IP addresses on top of to

00:14:00.081 --> 00:14:01.950
connect their VMs to

00:14:01.951 --> 00:14:03.240
their virtual machines
together and

00:14:03.241 --> 00:14:05.115
connect them with
our Azure services.

00:14:05.116 --> 00:14:06.590
Then, you can see we also as part

00:14:06.591 --> 00:14:08.010
of that network topology,

00:14:08.011 --> 00:14:10.535
a lot connection from
outside the data center.

00:14:10.536 --> 00:14:13.415
One of the examples
of a service that we

00:14:13.416 --> 00:14:15.270
make available to IT customers

00:14:15.271 --> 00:14:16.605
is something called ExpressRoute,

00:14:16.606 --> 00:14:18.360
which gives them
private connections into

00:14:18.361 --> 00:14:20.520
our dark fiber backbone
that gives them

00:14:20.521 --> 00:14:22.495
high SLAs up to 100 gigabits and

00:14:22.496 --> 00:14:24.830
dedicated bandwidth
into our backbone,

00:14:24.831 --> 00:14:27.765
and they don't have
to, then rely on

00:14:27.766 --> 00:14:32.190
that ISPs and the flakiness
of the open Internet.

00:14:32.191 --> 00:14:34.130
Speaking of that dark
fiber backbone,

00:14:34.131 --> 00:14:37.090
we've got one of the largest
networks in the world.

00:14:37.091 --> 00:14:42.070
Got about over 30,000 miles
of fiber spanning the world,

00:14:42.071 --> 00:14:44.960
you can see a map, this isn't
necessarily up to date,

00:14:44.961 --> 00:14:46.360
this is a few months old.

00:14:46.361 --> 00:14:47.740
But, you can get an idea of

00:14:47.741 --> 00:14:49.620
the kind of global
coverage we've got,

00:14:49.621 --> 00:14:52.700
including subsea cables
that we've laid ourselves.

00:14:52.701 --> 00:14:55.240
One of the examples
is one from Bilbao to

00:14:55.241 --> 00:14:56.670
Virginia called Maria that we

00:14:56.671 --> 00:14:58.545
launched a couple of years ago.

00:14:58.546 --> 00:15:02.225
One of the subsea cables is
a massive capital expense.

00:15:02.226 --> 00:15:05.670
We went into it with
Telsys and Facebook,

00:15:05.671 --> 00:15:07.690
but all three of us
recognizing that

00:15:07.691 --> 00:15:10.775
the existing subsea cables
connecting Europe and the US,

00:15:10.776 --> 00:15:13.755
almost all go from
England to New York,

00:15:13.756 --> 00:15:15.900
which causes a potential
single point of failure.

00:15:15.901 --> 00:15:17.505
So, in addition to building out

00:15:17.506 --> 00:15:19.510
an additional 150 terabits of

00:15:19.511 --> 00:15:21.970
capacity between
Europe and the US,

00:15:21.971 --> 00:15:24.770
this gives us some redundancy
with respect to

00:15:24.771 --> 00:15:26.380
those failure points of

00:15:26.381 --> 00:15:28.285
the existing cables that
you can see up there.

00:15:28.286 --> 00:15:30.695
We've also launched
another subsea cable

00:15:30.696 --> 00:15:34.700
connecting Asia and
the US as well recently.

00:15:34.701 --> 00:15:37.150
In addition to the dark fiber,

00:15:37.151 --> 00:15:39.475
we also have 4,500
peering connect

00:15:39.476 --> 00:15:40.850
locations to connect with

00:15:40.851 --> 00:15:42.145
other networks around the world,

00:15:42.146 --> 00:15:45.760
including ISPs, and then we
also have 130 Edge sites.

00:15:45.761 --> 00:15:47.030
The Edge sites are where we

00:15:47.031 --> 00:15:49.680
deploy our own
software and servers,

00:15:49.681 --> 00:15:51.330
where we can cache

00:15:51.331 --> 00:15:53.350
software and we can run
compute on the edge,

00:15:53.351 --> 00:15:55.450
do SSL termination and
give customers that

00:15:55.451 --> 00:15:57.850
are using our Cloud services
a better experience.

00:15:57.851 --> 00:16:01.580
We expect those point of
presence sites to also be part

00:16:01.581 --> 00:16:03.650
of our third party Edge strategy

00:16:03.651 --> 00:16:06.100
going forward, so
we're looking at that.

00:16:06.101 --> 00:16:09.080
Now, one of the aspects
of networking,

00:16:09.081 --> 00:16:11.060
I just focused on
the physical aspects

00:16:11.061 --> 00:16:13.295
in the previous slide is that,

00:16:13.296 --> 00:16:15.160
networking in the
Cloud is what's called

00:16:15.161 --> 00:16:17.210
software-defined
networking and what's

00:16:17.211 --> 00:16:19.420
off from many of you
probably are aware of this,

00:16:19.421 --> 00:16:22.400
software-defined networking
became something

00:16:22.401 --> 00:16:25.520
well-known about five
to seven years ago,

00:16:25.521 --> 00:16:27.680
we had companies being bought
for a billion dollars,

00:16:27.681 --> 00:16:28.660
because they were developing

00:16:28.661 --> 00:16:30.435
software-defined technologies.

00:16:30.436 --> 00:16:32.440
The idea here is
if you take a look

00:16:32.441 --> 00:16:34.594
at traditional IT networking,

00:16:34.595 --> 00:16:36.510
it's been an appliance model,

00:16:36.511 --> 00:16:39.050
where multiple layers of

00:16:39.051 --> 00:16:40.220
this networking stack are

00:16:40.221 --> 00:16:42.905
combined into
the single appliance.

00:16:42.906 --> 00:16:44.965
Those layers are
the management layer,

00:16:44.966 --> 00:16:47.230
which takes commands
for it to configure and

00:16:47.231 --> 00:16:51.355
deploy networks in
the data center.

00:16:51.356 --> 00:16:52.810
That control layer which

00:16:52.811 --> 00:16:54.490
actually takes
those abstractions that are

00:16:54.491 --> 00:16:55.870
exposed as APIs and actually

00:16:55.871 --> 00:16:57.960
translates them into things like,

00:16:57.961 --> 00:16:59.420
ACLs and routing rules,

00:16:59.421 --> 00:17:00.690
and then the data plane,

00:17:00.691 --> 00:17:02.810
which is programmed with
those ACLs and routing rules

00:17:02.811 --> 00:17:05.310
to honor what the control
plane decides,

00:17:05.311 --> 00:17:07.975
and the data flows through
the data plane, of course.

00:17:07.976 --> 00:17:10.770
With software-defined
networking, we

00:17:10.771 --> 00:17:13.375
take those and spread them out,

00:17:13.376 --> 00:17:15.810
and we implement them
all in software,

00:17:15.811 --> 00:17:17.080
at least mostly in software,

00:17:17.081 --> 00:17:19.000
I'll get to a caveat
on that in a second.

00:17:19.001 --> 00:17:21.480
If you take a look at the way
we've done this in Azure,

00:17:21.481 --> 00:17:23.470
the layers are the Azure
Resource Manager,

00:17:23.471 --> 00:17:24.740
which is the control and

00:17:24.741 --> 00:17:27.245
universal control plane
for all of the Azure APIs.

00:17:27.246 --> 00:17:28.490
If you talk to compute,

00:17:28.491 --> 00:17:31.250
if you talk to storage,
if you talk to Cosmos DB,

00:17:31.251 --> 00:17:33.165
on the control plane to create

00:17:33.166 --> 00:17:34.840
resources and to configure them,

00:17:34.841 --> 00:17:36.705
you're going through
Azure Resource Manager.

00:17:36.706 --> 00:17:38.435
So, when you create
a virtual network,

00:17:38.436 --> 00:17:40.100
you go through
Azure Resource Manager.

00:17:40.101 --> 00:17:41.650
Azure Resource Manager hands that

00:17:41.651 --> 00:17:43.765
off to the network
resource provider,

00:17:43.766 --> 00:17:45.800
which implements those APIs,

00:17:45.801 --> 00:17:47.655
and then that calls into

00:17:47.656 --> 00:17:50.230
network services
that are controllers

00:17:50.231 --> 00:17:51.715
to go and set up and

00:17:51.716 --> 00:17:55.230
define those virtual network
ACLs and routing rules,

00:17:55.231 --> 00:17:57.550
and then it talks to agents on

00:17:57.551 --> 00:18:01.595
the servers or the host
to implement those rules.

00:18:01.596 --> 00:18:04.805
The idea here is we want to
push as much logic down.

00:18:04.806 --> 00:18:07.250
The advantages of
software-defined networking,

00:18:07.251 --> 00:18:08.620
I kind of obvious here,

00:18:08.621 --> 00:18:11.324
we're not paying for expensive
proprietary appliances,

00:18:11.325 --> 00:18:14.990
which have reliability issues,

00:18:14.991 --> 00:18:16.670
they've got scalability issues.

00:18:16.671 --> 00:18:18.590
What we've got here is

00:18:18.591 --> 00:18:21.435
Cloud native layers of

00:18:21.436 --> 00:18:22.890
the networking stack
from the top to

00:18:22.891 --> 00:18:24.435
the bottom that we can expand,

00:18:24.436 --> 00:18:25.854
that are highly available,

00:18:25.855 --> 00:18:27.500
we can tolerate massive failures,

00:18:27.501 --> 00:18:29.940
including failures of
availability zones,

00:18:29.941 --> 00:18:33.770
and are much cheaper to operate.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.475
Now, if you take a look,

00:18:36.476 --> 00:18:39.040
I just mentioned Azure,

00:18:39.041 --> 00:18:40.220
the software-defined
networking stack in

00:18:40.221 --> 00:18:41.540
the context of Azure networking,

00:18:41.541 --> 00:18:44.940
you can see the layers here of

00:18:44.941 --> 00:18:47.390
the whole Azure Architecture
at a very high level.

00:18:47.391 --> 00:18:49.650
Up at the top there
on the far-right,

00:18:49.651 --> 00:18:51.130
you can see the Azure portal,

00:18:51.131 --> 00:18:52.200
that command line interface,

00:18:52.201 --> 00:18:56.930
these are and the SDKs which
talk these Management APIs,

00:18:56.931 --> 00:18:59.780
the control plane APIs to
Azure Resource Manager.

00:18:59.781 --> 00:19:02.010
Then, you can see
some examples services

00:19:02.011 --> 00:19:03.920
plugged into
Azure Resource Manager.

00:19:03.921 --> 00:19:06.645
We call those resource providers
like I just mentioned.

00:19:06.646 --> 00:19:08.160
Then, at beneath that,

00:19:08.161 --> 00:19:09.530
you've got the Azure
Fabric Controller,

00:19:09.531 --> 00:19:12.160
which is responsible for
launching virtual machines,

00:19:12.161 --> 00:19:14.950
everything except
for a few services

00:19:14.951 --> 00:19:17.090
at the very bottom of
our architectural stack,

00:19:17.091 --> 00:19:18.535
runs in virtual machines,

00:19:18.536 --> 00:19:20.480
including things like
Azure database and

00:19:20.481 --> 00:19:22.925
Cosmos database running
virtual machines.

00:19:22.926 --> 00:19:24.690
There's a hardware manager that's

00:19:24.691 --> 00:19:25.970
the manager's life cycle of

00:19:25.971 --> 00:19:27.600
servers and health
of the servers,

00:19:27.601 --> 00:19:29.430
and then the hardware
infrastructure underneath.

00:19:29.431 --> 00:19:32.660
Then, you can see that
there's authentication,

00:19:32.661 --> 00:19:34.210
role-based access
control, telemetry

00:19:34.211 --> 00:19:37.375
systems that span
the entire software stack.

00:19:37.376 --> 00:19:40.805
Now, if we focus just on
the networking part of this,

00:19:40.806 --> 00:19:45.380
this is a zoom in on what
I was explaining earlier,

00:19:45.381 --> 00:19:47.215
we've got Azure Resource Manager,

00:19:47.216 --> 00:19:48.380
the Network Resource Provider,

00:19:48.381 --> 00:19:49.480
and the Compute
Resource Provider.

00:19:49.481 --> 00:19:52.840
There's no network
without compute and so,

00:19:52.841 --> 00:19:55.330
these work closely together.

00:19:55.331 --> 00:19:57.890
They worked closely together
because a customer says,

00:19:57.891 --> 00:19:59.750
''I wanted to find
a virtual network and then I

00:19:59.751 --> 00:20:01.000
want to place a virtual machine

00:20:01.001 --> 00:20:02.340
into that virtual network.''

00:20:02.341 --> 00:20:04.290
The job then of the system is to

00:20:04.291 --> 00:20:05.950
go find a computer to put

00:20:05.951 --> 00:20:07.500
that virtual machine on and

00:20:07.501 --> 00:20:10.030
then join that to
the virtual network.

00:20:10.031 --> 00:20:11.880
So, what you'll see is that,

00:20:11.881 --> 00:20:14.665
these components all
meet down on the server,

00:20:14.666 --> 00:20:16.885
each talking to their own agents.

00:20:16.886 --> 00:20:19.175
There's a network agent
down on the server,

00:20:19.176 --> 00:20:20.480
there's a compute agent
on the server

00:20:20.481 --> 00:20:21.985
that launches a virtual machine.

00:20:21.986 --> 00:20:25.640
The network agent then
plums in IP addresses and

00:20:25.641 --> 00:20:27.570
routing rules into that
virtual machine to have

00:20:27.571 --> 00:20:30.500
it join this virtual network.

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:34.890
So, another area where we've

00:20:34.891 --> 00:20:36.160
collaborated with
Microsoft Research,

00:20:36.161 --> 00:20:38.570
and this one goes
back to a project

00:20:38.571 --> 00:20:41.545
that started in MSR about 2010.

00:20:41.546 --> 00:20:44.350
Look one of the MSR researchers,

00:20:44.351 --> 00:20:45.710
Doug Burger, who now is part of

00:20:45.711 --> 00:20:47.460
the Azure team in
the hardware group.

00:20:47.461 --> 00:20:51.870
He had this vision that
FPGAs had a role in

00:20:51.871 --> 00:20:53.645
the modern data
center architecture

00:20:53.646 --> 00:20:55.320
and FPGAs have come and gone

00:20:55.321 --> 00:20:57.140
throughout time and they

00:20:57.141 --> 00:20:59.590
fell out of vote
for over a decade.

00:20:59.591 --> 00:21:01.440
Doug believed that they had

00:21:01.441 --> 00:21:04.560
a place and he took his vision to

00:21:04.561 --> 00:21:08.550
Bing and started a project

00:21:08.551 --> 00:21:09.430
with Bing to see if they could

00:21:09.431 --> 00:21:10.810
accelerate the index serving,

00:21:10.811 --> 00:21:13.840
the indexing of being
content on FPGAs.

00:21:13.841 --> 00:21:16.230
That project pilot was

00:21:16.231 --> 00:21:18.900
successful and then deployed
into production in Bing.

00:21:18.901 --> 00:21:21.355
Now, we faced an issue

00:21:21.356 --> 00:21:24.060
in Azure about five
or six years ago,

00:21:24.061 --> 00:21:26.420
where we saw networks going from

00:21:26.421 --> 00:21:29.535
1 gigabit to 10 gigabit
on the servers.

00:21:29.536 --> 00:21:31.345
We saw 40 gigabit coming,

00:21:31.346 --> 00:21:33.395
50 gigabits coming next year,

00:21:33.396 --> 00:21:35.645
100 gigabits around the corner.

00:21:35.646 --> 00:21:38.800
When we took a look at us

00:21:38.801 --> 00:21:41.980
processing that amount of data
on the servers themselves,

00:21:41.981 --> 00:21:43.060
on the compute servers,

00:21:43.061 --> 00:21:46.200
on the CPUs, we realized
that we'd be burning

00:21:46.201 --> 00:21:48.470
large amounts of those computers

00:21:48.471 --> 00:21:50.590
just doing nothing,
but packet processing.

00:21:50.591 --> 00:21:51.980
So the question is, how

00:21:51.981 --> 00:21:53.470
could we do this
more efficiently?

00:21:53.471 --> 00:21:56.390
How could we keep up not
just with the bandwidth,

00:21:56.391 --> 00:22:01.810
but keep latency low for
that amount of data processing?

00:22:01.811 --> 00:22:05.430
Our network engineers found
out about the Bing project,

00:22:05.431 --> 00:22:07.650
Doug found out about
our problem and we started to

00:22:07.651 --> 00:22:08.910
collaborate together on something

00:22:08.911 --> 00:22:10.500
called accelerated networking.

00:22:10.501 --> 00:22:12.860
The idea here is that
we take those FPGAs and

00:22:12.861 --> 00:22:15.645
just a quick refresh on
FPGAs for those of you that

00:22:15.646 --> 00:22:17.730
might not have been around for

00:22:17.731 --> 00:22:19.705
those previous phases or

00:22:19.706 --> 00:22:22.665
just know it from
the acronym there.

00:22:22.666 --> 00:22:26.190
There's small devices with

00:22:26.191 --> 00:22:28.355
tens to thousands
of logic elements,

00:22:28.356 --> 00:22:30.010
typically DSPs on them,

00:22:30.011 --> 00:22:34.200
interfaces to the PCIE bus,
USB controller.

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:37.910
The idea here, is that
the interconnect between them is

00:22:37.911 --> 00:22:39.169
dynamically programmable

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:40.730
within a very short
period of time.

00:22:40.731 --> 00:22:41.880
Within a few milliseconds,

00:22:41.881 --> 00:22:45.280
you can program the
interconnects between these and

00:22:45.281 --> 00:22:46.780
what that allows
you to do is create

00:22:46.781 --> 00:22:49.035
a reconfigurable topology.

00:22:49.036 --> 00:22:50.740
These kinds of devices because

00:22:50.741 --> 00:22:54.250
there're so much redundancy here

00:22:54.251 --> 00:22:56.060
of the same kinds of computational
elements that can be

00:22:56.061 --> 00:22:59.230
programmed are great for
highly parallel workloads,

00:22:59.231 --> 00:23:01.795
and packet processing is
a highly parallel workload.

00:23:01.796 --> 00:23:04.830
So, looking at these,
we said, "Hey,

00:23:04.831 --> 00:23:09.300
maybe we can plumb
the SDN stack into an FPGA."

00:23:09.301 --> 00:23:12.885
Effectively, getting close to
hardware ASIC performance,

00:23:12.886 --> 00:23:14.050
but with the flexibility of

00:23:14.051 --> 00:23:15.540
being able to update
the algorithms in

00:23:15.541 --> 00:23:18.605
the software-defined
networking as we go.

00:23:18.606 --> 00:23:22.260
In fact, we have added
many, many features,

00:23:22.261 --> 00:23:23.620
dozens of features just in

00:23:23.621 --> 00:23:24.740
the last couple of years to

00:23:24.741 --> 00:23:26.425
our software-defined
networking stack.

00:23:26.426 --> 00:23:28.590
So, we absolutely need
that flexibility.

00:23:28.591 --> 00:23:32.140
We cannot burn the software-defined
networking algorithms

00:23:32.141 --> 00:23:34.150
into ASICs because
that would just not

00:23:34.151 --> 00:23:37.300
allow us to let the network
at large evolve.

00:23:37.301 --> 00:23:39.190
So, what we did is,

00:23:39.191 --> 00:23:40.640
we figured out how to plumb

00:23:40.641 --> 00:23:42.665
the networking stack
into the FPGA,

00:23:42.666 --> 00:23:44.310
so that in the previous slide,

00:23:44.311 --> 00:23:46.740
where you talk about the
networking agent on the servers,

00:23:46.741 --> 00:23:49.465
getting those control
plane rules for

00:23:49.466 --> 00:23:53.275
ACLs and for routes
and for IP addresses,

00:23:53.276 --> 00:23:55.865
it plumbs those into

00:23:55.866 --> 00:23:57.480
flow tables that are implemented

00:23:57.481 --> 00:24:00.550
in soft logic on those FPGAs.

00:24:00.551 --> 00:24:02.710
You can see the comparison as we

00:24:02.711 --> 00:24:04.595
moved into accelerated networks,

00:24:04.596 --> 00:24:06.100
we started deploying FPGAs and

00:24:06.101 --> 00:24:08.375
every single server
about three years ago,

00:24:08.376 --> 00:24:10.670
so now, we think we have

00:24:10.671 --> 00:24:12.010
the most deployed FPGAs in

00:24:12.011 --> 00:24:13.540
production of any company
in the world.

00:24:13.541 --> 00:24:17.050
The left side of

00:24:17.051 --> 00:24:18.810
that slide shows you
the software architecture,

00:24:18.811 --> 00:24:21.900
where we're processing those
packets on the host on

00:24:21.901 --> 00:24:23.770
the servers and you
can see there's

00:24:23.771 --> 00:24:25.920
a lot of software layers
that we've got to go to.

00:24:25.921 --> 00:24:27.475
On the right side, you can see,

00:24:27.476 --> 00:24:28.955
we bypass all that,

00:24:28.956 --> 00:24:32.545
allow the virtual machine
to talk directly to

00:24:32.546 --> 00:24:36.065
a network interface
that's virtualized and

00:24:36.066 --> 00:24:38.170
provided directly to
that virtual machine,

00:24:38.171 --> 00:24:39.920
so that they can just
talk directly to

00:24:39.921 --> 00:24:41.410
the hardware and so there's

00:24:41.411 --> 00:24:43.620
no computational
resources other than

00:24:43.621 --> 00:24:46.925
some control plane
operations on the CPUs.

00:24:46.926 --> 00:24:49.825
That frees up the CPUs
for of course to do,

00:24:49.826 --> 00:24:51.510
more useful things
like actually run

00:24:51.511 --> 00:24:54.155
virtual machines instead
of process packets.

00:24:54.156 --> 00:24:57.630
So, I'm going to show you
a quick demo of comparison

00:24:57.631 --> 00:25:01.540
between those two different
technologies here in action.

00:25:01.541 --> 00:25:04.860
First, I've got
two virtual machines here

00:25:04.861 --> 00:25:07.500
that I've got without accelerated
networking turned on.

00:25:07.501 --> 00:25:08.800
Accelerated networking
is available in

00:25:08.801 --> 00:25:10.835
all virtual machines
around the world in Azure,

00:25:10.836 --> 00:25:12.555
it's an opt-in currently.

00:25:12.556 --> 00:25:14.930
Because we wanted to make
sure that there were

00:25:14.931 --> 00:25:17.705
no compatibility issues with

00:25:17.706 --> 00:25:19.825
operating systems
that customers use.

00:25:19.826 --> 00:25:21.880
So right now, it's opt-in,

00:25:21.881 --> 00:25:24.055
soon it will be opt-out.

00:25:24.056 --> 00:25:26.490
But on this virtual machine,

00:25:26.491 --> 00:25:29.160
I have it opted out.

00:25:29.161 --> 00:25:30.570
What I've done is just set up

00:25:30.571 --> 00:25:34.040
a receiver with a program
called "Sockperf."

00:25:34.041 --> 00:25:35.670
Here's another
virtual machine also

00:25:35.671 --> 00:25:37.140
opted out that will talk to

00:25:37.141 --> 00:25:40.530
that other one and send it
packets as quickly as it can.

00:25:40.531 --> 00:25:42.880
What we're going to see after
about 15 seconds of run,

00:25:42.881 --> 00:25:44.385
is the latency that

00:25:44.386 --> 00:25:45.940
these two virtual
machines experience

00:25:45.941 --> 00:25:47.370
talking to one another over

00:25:47.371 --> 00:25:49.300
that software-defined
networking stack as

00:25:49.301 --> 00:25:52.670
they're implemented in the host.

00:25:52.671 --> 00:25:57.375
So you can see the latency,

00:25:57.376 --> 00:26:01.655
it's about 200 microseconds,
which isn't bad.

00:26:01.656 --> 00:26:02.850
But let's go take a look at

00:26:02.851 --> 00:26:05.330
what accelerated
networking can give us.

00:26:05.331 --> 00:26:08.620
So, I've got here,

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:11.370
another program and
this is using something

00:26:11.371 --> 00:26:13.670
called DPDK on a Linux VM,

00:26:13.671 --> 00:26:17.150
which allows the Linux
kernel to process packets

00:26:17.151 --> 00:26:19.630
extremely fast with
very low latency,

00:26:19.631 --> 00:26:21.740
so we're going to
see something that

00:26:21.741 --> 00:26:25.040
looks a little bit better.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:33.670
So, that's 10 microseconds

00:26:33.671 --> 00:26:35.620
and so we see typically latencies

00:26:35.621 --> 00:26:37.600
between 5 and 10 microseconds
as it compared to

00:26:37.601 --> 00:26:40.135
about 200 microseconds on
software-defined stack.

00:26:40.136 --> 00:26:42.925
Again, with almost no CPU usage

00:26:42.926 --> 00:26:46.135
on that versus
the software stack.

00:26:46.136 --> 00:26:50.170
Okay, let's switch
back. All right.

00:26:50.171 --> 00:26:52.105
So let's talk a little bit
about servers now.

00:26:52.106 --> 00:26:54.840
I'm going to take you
on a tour of history

00:26:54.841 --> 00:26:58.410
here to show you the evolution
of servers in the Cloud.

00:26:58.411 --> 00:27:00.230
Because it's kind of interesting

00:27:00.231 --> 00:27:01.870
back when I started in Azure,

00:27:01.871 --> 00:27:03.765
the whole premise of Cloud

00:27:03.766 --> 00:27:07.020
was cheap commodity
scale out server.

00:27:07.021 --> 00:27:10.005
Scale up was anathema to Cloud,

00:27:10.006 --> 00:27:11.905
but watch what happens.

00:27:11.906 --> 00:27:14.080
Here's our Generation
Two servers that we

00:27:14.081 --> 00:27:16.345
launched Azure within 2010.

00:27:16.346 --> 00:27:18.780
There are two socket Opterons

00:27:18.781 --> 00:27:21.780
with six CPUs on each socket,

00:27:21.781 --> 00:27:23.160
so a total of 12 cores,

00:27:23.161 --> 00:27:24.680
has 32 gigabytes of RAM and

00:27:24.681 --> 00:27:26.155
has 1 gigabit network adapter.

00:27:26.156 --> 00:27:28.205
I'm going to quickly
move through these.

00:27:28.206 --> 00:27:30.830
Gen Three, we jumped
to 10 gigabits.

00:27:30.831 --> 00:27:34.060
Here's our HPC scheme we
started to introduce.

00:27:34.061 --> 00:27:35.960
What we did here
with our HPC SKUs,

00:27:35.961 --> 00:27:37.410
which we still have
is Infiniband,

00:27:37.411 --> 00:27:38.990
40 gigabit, Infiniband
networking,

00:27:38.991 --> 00:27:41.460
which offers latencies
that are even down

00:27:41.461 --> 00:27:44.145
to 1 to 2 microseconds as
opposed to what we see there.

00:27:44.146 --> 00:27:47.435
By the way, that was
TCP over Infiniband.

00:27:47.436 --> 00:27:52.000
It's of course a different type
of network protocol,

00:27:52.001 --> 00:27:54.245
it's not TCP compatible.

00:27:54.246 --> 00:27:55.860
Here, we have our Gen
Four servers,

00:27:55.861 --> 00:27:57.280
you can see that
the amount of RAM

00:27:57.281 --> 00:27:59.010
that we're starting
to deploy is larger.

00:27:59.011 --> 00:28:01.110
There's more CPUs,
and this lets us pack

00:28:01.111 --> 00:28:03.995
more virtual machines
onto the same server.

00:28:03.996 --> 00:28:06.295
Then, we introduced
something called Godzilla,

00:28:06.296 --> 00:28:08.860
and here's the first example
of a massive scale up,

00:28:08.861 --> 00:28:11.820
this was about three years
ago and what we saw,

00:28:11.821 --> 00:28:13.505
customers starting to migrate

00:28:13.506 --> 00:28:16.175
large in-memory databases
to the Cloud.

00:28:16.176 --> 00:28:18.970
Specifically SAP HANA, one
of the core workloads in

00:28:18.971 --> 00:28:22.625
IT is a scale up in
memory database,

00:28:22.626 --> 00:28:25.830
and they needed very large
virtual machines to be able to

00:28:25.831 --> 00:28:27.190
support the kinds of

00:28:27.191 --> 00:28:28.850
computation that
their enterprises ran on.

00:28:28.851 --> 00:28:30.665
So, we introduced this,

00:28:30.666 --> 00:28:32.105
that's called the Godzilla.

00:28:32.106 --> 00:28:35.420
It has 512 megabytes of memory,

00:28:35.421 --> 00:28:37.570
so the largest virtual machine,

00:28:37.571 --> 00:28:42.740
and these have
450 gigabytes of memory.

00:28:42.770 --> 00:28:45.320
Here's our Gen 5.1.

00:28:45.321 --> 00:28:48.410
So, this is what we've been
deploying for last few years.

00:28:48.411 --> 00:28:49.900
Then you can see,
we have started to

00:28:49.901 --> 00:28:51.360
introduce GPUs and these are

00:28:51.361 --> 00:28:54.940
NVIDIA GPUs P100s, P40s.

00:28:54.941 --> 00:28:59.490
Then, we introduced this
about a year and a half ago,

00:28:59.491 --> 00:29:01.740
this we called Beast.

00:29:01.741 --> 00:29:04.460
This one, again,
customers migrating

00:29:04.461 --> 00:29:06.240
even more and larger
SAP workloads

00:29:06.241 --> 00:29:07.975
to the Cloud including
Microsoft IT,

00:29:07.976 --> 00:29:10.075
we needed even larger
virtual machines.

00:29:10.076 --> 00:29:12.050
This one has 4 terabytes
of RAM in it,

00:29:12.051 --> 00:29:14.950
has 144 processors in it.

00:29:14.951 --> 00:29:17.660
So, this now is what we've got

00:29:17.661 --> 00:29:20.955
currently in production
as our largest VM size.

00:29:20.956 --> 00:29:22.975
You can see our Gen Six server,

00:29:22.976 --> 00:29:26.270
this is what we're deploying
right now into production,

00:29:26.271 --> 00:29:29.095
and you can see it's got
768 gigabytes of RAM in it.

00:29:29.096 --> 00:29:31.050
So, we continue to rise up on RAM

00:29:31.051 --> 00:29:33.110
and CPUs and what we just

00:29:33.111 --> 00:29:38.040
announced about
a month ago, is this.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:41.710
We called it Beast B2 internally,

00:29:41.711 --> 00:29:43.485
but I think we should
call it Mega Beast.

00:29:43.486 --> 00:29:46.360
This thing has
12 terabytes of RAM in it.

00:29:46.361 --> 00:29:48.010
So, we're the first
public Cloud to announce

00:29:48.011 --> 00:29:51.040
support for 12 terabyte
virtual machines.

00:29:51.041 --> 00:29:53.230
That of course, SAP

00:29:53.231 --> 00:29:55.980
workload unblocking as
they move to the Cloud.

00:29:55.981 --> 00:29:59.755
So not quite commodity
scale out anymore.

00:29:59.756 --> 00:30:01.990
Well, all of this
is built on top of

00:30:01.991 --> 00:30:04.520
an open source platform
called Project Olympus.

00:30:04.521 --> 00:30:06.990
Project Olympus is
designed to be extremely

00:30:06.991 --> 00:30:09.260
flexible to support
high density storage,

00:30:09.261 --> 00:30:10.980
to support SSD, hard disks,

00:30:10.981 --> 00:30:13.130
also to support
all the different kinds of

00:30:13.131 --> 00:30:15.500
processor technologies we
want to put into the cloud,

00:30:15.501 --> 00:30:19.010
including AMD, ARM, as
well as Intel processors.

00:30:19.011 --> 00:30:21.375
We also support NVDIMM.

00:30:21.376 --> 00:30:23.515
So, we've support batteries

00:30:23.516 --> 00:30:25.710
in these servers that power RAM,

00:30:25.711 --> 00:30:27.240
so that we can tolerate

00:30:27.241 --> 00:30:30.275
a power outage but treat
that RAM as non-volatile,

00:30:30.276 --> 00:30:32.145
being able to write
it out to a flash

00:30:32.146 --> 00:30:35.185
before the server
completely loses power.

00:30:35.186 --> 00:30:38.380
This is designed to go
to 50 gigabit NICs.

00:30:38.381 --> 00:30:40.110
Like I said, we're
already looking at

00:30:40.111 --> 00:30:42.950
100 gigabit coming at us.

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:45.985
Project Olympus actually is

00:30:45.986 --> 00:30:48.260
open as we contributed
the open compute projects.

00:30:48.261 --> 00:30:49.340
So, anybody can go look at

00:30:49.341 --> 00:30:50.830
these designs and build
their own servers,

00:30:50.831 --> 00:30:55.535
the idea is being that we
innovate with the ecosystem.

00:30:55.536 --> 00:30:58.700
In fact, Olympus Gen Six

00:30:58.701 --> 00:31:02.640
was the first version
of our server design,

00:31:02.641 --> 00:31:05.670
that we actually took
the initial design out,

00:31:05.671 --> 00:31:07.030
made it available publicly and

00:31:07.031 --> 00:31:09.365
sought feedback from
all our hardware partners,

00:31:09.366 --> 00:31:12.680
and so they all made contributions
back into it that were

00:31:12.681 --> 00:31:15.000
then eventually made it into

00:31:15.001 --> 00:31:18.510
the final Gen Six Project
Olympus platform.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:21.280
Now, we're also looking at

00:31:21.281 --> 00:31:23.620
introducing specialized hardware
into our data centers.

00:31:23.621 --> 00:31:25.530
We've done things like we've
announced in Cerberus,

00:31:25.531 --> 00:31:27.380
a specialized
security co-processor

00:31:27.381 --> 00:31:28.660
that we put into our servers.

00:31:28.661 --> 00:31:30.540
But another place
that we're looking

00:31:30.541 --> 00:31:32.510
at is accelerating deep learning.

00:31:32.511 --> 00:31:35.180
This is another project
led by Doug Burger

00:31:35.181 --> 00:31:39.970
focusing on the use of FPGAs
for deep neural networks.

00:31:39.971 --> 00:31:43.190
His idea here is again,

00:31:43.191 --> 00:31:46.365
machine learning technology
is changing very quickly.

00:31:46.366 --> 00:31:49.870
So, instead of burning
topologies into A-six and

00:31:49.871 --> 00:31:54.030
having them be static and rigid
and have short lifetimes,

00:31:54.031 --> 00:31:55.790
if we can leverage FPGAs,

00:31:55.791 --> 00:31:57.080
we could adapt to

00:31:57.081 --> 00:31:59.630
new algorithms as they
come up new topologies.

00:31:59.631 --> 00:32:01.780
So, the first version of this

00:32:01.781 --> 00:32:03.910
is called Project
Brainwave and we

00:32:03.911 --> 00:32:05.820
released this publicly in

00:32:05.821 --> 00:32:08.835
the public preview at
Build earlier this year.

00:32:08.836 --> 00:32:10.600
The idea here, of course,

00:32:10.601 --> 00:32:13.405
is that we can plumb
these DNNs into FPGAs.

00:32:13.406 --> 00:32:15.400
These FPGAs are on
servers that are

00:32:15.401 --> 00:32:17.140
connected with a
low-latency network and so,

00:32:17.141 --> 00:32:19.290
we can actually take
very large DNNs that don't

00:32:19.291 --> 00:32:21.590
fit into a single FPGA
and deploy them

00:32:21.591 --> 00:32:24.440
across multiple servers
to have them

00:32:24.441 --> 00:32:27.710
operate as basically
a hardware is a service,

00:32:27.711 --> 00:32:31.020
micro-service platform,
that's Doug's vision.

00:32:31.021 --> 00:32:32.430
Now, what we've done,

00:32:32.431 --> 00:32:34.080
I'm going to talk
a little bit about one of

00:32:34.081 --> 00:32:37.135
the specific use cases
here and that is,

00:32:37.136 --> 00:32:39.705
something called AI for Earth,

00:32:39.706 --> 00:32:42.660
collaboration between us, ESRI,

00:32:42.661 --> 00:32:46.280
and the Chesapeake Conservatory
to go look at land

00:32:46.281 --> 00:32:48.830
and evaluate how it's changing

00:32:48.831 --> 00:32:51.680
with respect to climate
change and other factors.

00:32:51.681 --> 00:32:54.180
Traditionally, this has
been done by humans

00:32:54.181 --> 00:32:56.210
and satellite imageries
is captured and then

00:32:56.211 --> 00:32:58.060
humans go and look
at these images

00:32:58.061 --> 00:33:00.130
and decide how much forestry,

00:33:00.131 --> 00:33:03.310
how much water,
how much urban space is

00:33:03.311 --> 00:33:06.660
in the image and then they
track that over time.

00:33:06.661 --> 00:33:08.195
So, hundreds of hours,

00:33:08.196 --> 00:33:12.385
thousands of hours spent by
humans up to now on this.

00:33:12.386 --> 00:33:15.350
Now, with Brainwave, we
can take those images,

00:33:15.351 --> 00:33:18.460
the first algorithm
neural network

00:33:18.461 --> 00:33:21.550
that we programmed into
Brainwave is resonant 50.

00:33:21.551 --> 00:33:23.380
It's customizable resonant 50

00:33:23.381 --> 00:33:24.600
where the customer can train

00:33:24.601 --> 00:33:27.660
their own model as
the final layer and then

00:33:27.661 --> 00:33:30.410
package that up even and
deploy it on on-premise

00:33:30.411 --> 00:33:33.560
Brainwave but it's deployed
as a service in Azure.

00:33:33.561 --> 00:33:34.660
Once the customer trains

00:33:34.661 --> 00:33:36.495
their model on top
of resonant 50,

00:33:36.496 --> 00:33:40.200
they deploy it into Brainwave
VMs and then can access it

00:33:40.201 --> 00:33:43.710
through via an API and that's
exactly what this has done.

00:33:43.711 --> 00:33:45.190
This pipeline shows you.

00:33:45.191 --> 00:33:46.840
I'm going to switch
to a demo to show

00:33:46.841 --> 00:33:50.140
you Brainwave in action.

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:54.030
So, first I'm going to show you

00:33:54.031 --> 00:33:57.945
this Explorer land
cover classification

00:33:57.946 --> 00:34:00.055
which will show us.

00:34:00.056 --> 00:34:02.610
If I just select one of these,

00:34:02.611 --> 00:34:06.000
it's going to execute it on
Brainwave and inference.

00:34:06.001 --> 00:34:08.800
Now, Brainwave can infer,

00:34:08.801 --> 00:34:11.110
do inference of an image in

00:34:11.111 --> 00:34:13.880
less than two
milliseconds and it can

00:34:13.881 --> 00:34:16.955
drive throughput up
to 40 teraflops,

00:34:16.956 --> 00:34:19.765
tera operations
off an FPGA device

00:34:19.766 --> 00:34:21.290
even at batch size of one.

00:34:21.291 --> 00:34:22.970
So, this is what we
call real time AI.

00:34:22.971 --> 00:34:24.545
Something that is
differentiated from

00:34:24.546 --> 00:34:28.325
other traditional batch
oriented devices like a GPU,

00:34:28.326 --> 00:34:31.135
where you've got to feed in 100,

00:34:31.136 --> 00:34:33.465
200 images at once

00:34:33.466 --> 00:34:36.155
to get that kind of
throughput out of the device.

00:34:36.156 --> 00:34:40.160
Now, here's the AI for Earth app
and what I'm going to do

00:34:40.161 --> 00:34:43.770
is process these
images using CPU.

00:34:43.771 --> 00:34:48.365
You can see the CPU's processing
about six a second,

00:34:48.366 --> 00:34:52.425
batch oriented GPU about 60.

00:34:52.426 --> 00:34:55.310
You can see it's
progress down here

00:34:55.311 --> 00:34:59.230
as it classifies those images
and then here's Brainwave.

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:02.285
Then, like I mentioned,

00:35:02.286 --> 00:35:03.980
we can scale up Brainwave.

00:35:03.981 --> 00:35:11.470
So, here's Brainwave
with roughly 800 FPGAs.

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:15.820
So, the total dataset for

00:35:15.821 --> 00:35:19.730
the US is about 195
million images,

00:35:19.731 --> 00:35:21.050
20 terabytes of data.

00:35:21.051 --> 00:35:24.010
With Brainwave, we can
process it in 10 minutes.

00:35:24.011 --> 00:35:26.230
The entire dataset for a cost of

00:35:26.231 --> 00:35:30.500
about $43. Okay,
let's switch back.

00:35:30.501 --> 00:35:36.890
So, let's talk a little bit

00:35:36.891 --> 00:35:39.910
about compute now and

00:35:39.911 --> 00:35:41.340
here's that high-level Azure

00:35:41.341 --> 00:35:43.000
architecture diagram that
I showed you earlier.

00:35:43.001 --> 00:35:45.670
What we're going
to focus on now is

00:35:45.671 --> 00:35:49.480
the compute stack
and specifically,

00:35:49.481 --> 00:35:53.260
how we deploy multiple workloads
down onto the platform.

00:35:53.261 --> 00:35:54.775
The Azure fabric controller,

00:35:54.776 --> 00:35:55.910
like I said, is responsible

00:35:55.911 --> 00:35:57.600
for launching virtual machines,

00:35:57.601 --> 00:35:59.250
but that's typically
not the way that

00:35:59.251 --> 00:36:01.970
customers using
cloud native technologies

00:36:01.971 --> 00:36:03.590
will interact with the platform.

00:36:03.591 --> 00:36:05.540
They will, of course,

00:36:05.541 --> 00:36:06.850
use virtual machines to migrate

00:36:06.851 --> 00:36:08.490
their existing IT
workloads in a lift and

00:36:08.491 --> 00:36:10.090
shift motion but if

00:36:10.091 --> 00:36:11.600
they're building
something cloud native,

00:36:11.601 --> 00:36:13.045
they're going to be
using containers

00:36:13.046 --> 00:36:14.705
and container orchestrators.

00:36:14.706 --> 00:36:16.540
So, that's what you can see here.

00:36:16.541 --> 00:36:18.790
A couple of container
orchestrators is kind of the ones

00:36:18.791 --> 00:36:21.400
that we have bet on.

00:36:21.401 --> 00:36:24.175
One is called AKS Azure
Container Service

00:36:24.176 --> 00:36:26.065
which is Kubernetes as a service.

00:36:26.066 --> 00:36:29.775
Kubernetes being the Open
Source Container orchestrator.

00:36:29.776 --> 00:36:31.800
It's open source.

00:36:31.801 --> 00:36:33.670
It was developed at
Google by three people.

00:36:33.671 --> 00:36:34.895
In fact, in one of them,

00:36:34.896 --> 00:36:37.725
Brendan Burns, actually works
in the Azure compute team.

00:36:37.726 --> 00:36:41.210
So, Azure, we leverage
open source a lot.

00:36:41.211 --> 00:36:42.690
We contribute to open
source a lot and we've

00:36:42.691 --> 00:36:43.190
gained a lot of

00:36:43.191 --> 00:36:44.480
credibility with
the open source community

00:36:44.481 --> 00:36:47.955
enough that often times,

00:36:47.956 --> 00:36:49.740
we see people wanting to
leave Google to come to

00:36:49.741 --> 00:36:51.780
Microsoft to work on
open-source technologies.

00:36:51.781 --> 00:36:54.220
Kind of something
that is shocking

00:36:54.221 --> 00:36:55.440
for people that have
watched Microsoft

00:36:55.441 --> 00:36:56.975
over the last two decades.

00:36:56.976 --> 00:36:58.740
Now, the other service you

00:36:58.741 --> 00:37:00.100
can see here is called
Service Fabric.

00:37:00.101 --> 00:37:02.470
This is one technology
we developed internally

00:37:02.471 --> 00:37:05.640
and that most of our cloud
services are built on top of.

00:37:05.641 --> 00:37:08.010
Most of the Azure infrastructure
is built on top of

00:37:08.011 --> 00:37:10.110
this micro-service platform which

00:37:10.111 --> 00:37:12.079
can also do container
orchestration.

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:16.390
But it's unique in that it
supports state-full services.

00:37:16.391 --> 00:37:19.145
So, it's tightly integrated.

00:37:19.146 --> 00:37:20.710
It's federation model,

00:37:20.711 --> 00:37:22.530
it's help model to be
highly scalable and to

00:37:22.531 --> 00:37:28.120
support the highly
durable storage of data.

00:37:28.121 --> 00:37:29.880
When you take a look
at a service like

00:37:29.881 --> 00:37:32.035
Cosmos DB which is
built on top of this,

00:37:32.036 --> 00:37:34.685
Cosmos DB is replicating data,

00:37:34.686 --> 00:37:37.400
through copies of each database.

00:37:37.401 --> 00:37:39.835
So, with tight coordination with

00:37:39.836 --> 00:37:43.850
the Service Fabric controller
on health, on upgrades,

00:37:43.851 --> 00:37:45.970
Service Fabric can
make sure that that

00:37:45.971 --> 00:37:47.840
data is always available for

00:37:47.841 --> 00:37:49.890
right operations in the face

00:37:49.891 --> 00:37:52.810
of failures and
re-configurations.

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:59.925
Service Fabric is something
that if you take a look,

00:37:59.926 --> 00:38:01.880
actually, let me back up here,

00:38:01.881 --> 00:38:03.875
and I'll come back to
service fabric in a second.

00:38:03.876 --> 00:38:05.455
A deeper dive into,

00:38:05.456 --> 00:38:06.530
I thought I'd show you just the

00:38:06.531 --> 00:38:08.220
complexity that we've gotten to,

00:38:08.221 --> 00:38:11.100
in orchestration of something
that seems as simple as

00:38:11.101 --> 00:38:14.065
deploying a virtual
machine in the cloud.

00:38:14.066 --> 00:38:16.260
Here, you can see all of
the resource providers,

00:38:16.261 --> 00:38:18.155
or at least, most of
the resource providers,

00:38:18.156 --> 00:38:20.260
and some of the services regional

00:38:20.261 --> 00:38:21.520
services that are involved

00:38:21.521 --> 00:38:23.155
with the deployment
of virtual machine,

00:38:23.156 --> 00:38:26.565
which includes compute
network, and storage.

00:38:26.566 --> 00:38:30.130
You can see we've got
the idea of clusters down,

00:38:30.131 --> 00:38:31.690
clusters with cluster managers

00:38:31.691 --> 00:38:33.555
that manage groups of servers.

00:38:33.556 --> 00:38:36.365
So, tenant manager that's TM,

00:38:36.366 --> 00:38:39.310
data center manager manages
the hardware for cluster,

00:38:39.311 --> 00:38:42.220
network service manager,
software load balancer,

00:38:42.221 --> 00:38:45.710
all of those are basically
cluster level services.

00:38:45.711 --> 00:38:49.140
They manage a slice of
an entire data center or region,

00:38:49.141 --> 00:38:50.800
and then down on the servers,

00:38:50.801 --> 00:38:53.165
we've got agents corresponding
to each of these.

00:38:53.166 --> 00:38:54.710
When we deploy a virtual machine,

00:38:54.711 --> 00:38:56.895
here's the flows that you see,

00:38:56.896 --> 00:38:59.175
the service is talking
to each other,

00:38:59.176 --> 00:39:02.160
communicating data, so that
we can finally end up with

00:39:02.161 --> 00:39:03.725
a virtual machine
down on a server

00:39:03.726 --> 00:39:06.390
that has a network and
storage attached to it.

00:39:06.391 --> 00:39:09.110
But going back to Service Fabric,

00:39:09.111 --> 00:39:11.085
which is layered on top of this,

00:39:11.086 --> 00:39:12.740
where the world is going is

00:39:12.741 --> 00:39:14.390
something called
serverless technology.

00:39:14.391 --> 00:39:15.580
So, we went from containers

00:39:15.581 --> 00:39:16.970
a few years ago which
was all the rage,

00:39:16.971 --> 00:39:20.010
then to contain orchestrators
and microservice platforms,

00:39:20.011 --> 00:39:22.450
and pass on top of microservices.

00:39:22.451 --> 00:39:25.975
Then now, what we see
is the next level

00:39:25.976 --> 00:39:27.660
of abstraction of
productivity which is

00:39:27.661 --> 00:39:29.515
built on top of
serverless technologies.

00:39:29.516 --> 00:39:32.795
Serverless, really pioneered
by functions as a service.

00:39:32.796 --> 00:39:34.120
We've got our own functions as

00:39:34.121 --> 00:39:35.820
a service called Azure Functions.

00:39:35.821 --> 00:39:37.130
The idea here being the developer

00:39:37.131 --> 00:39:38.490
just writes a piece of code,

00:39:38.491 --> 00:39:39.900
then give it to
the Cloud platform,

00:39:39.901 --> 00:39:41.080
and we take care of
everything else.

00:39:41.081 --> 00:39:42.525
We take care of Auto scaling,

00:39:42.526 --> 00:39:45.960
we take care of
the reliability of it.

00:39:45.961 --> 00:39:47.740
They focus just on the code,

00:39:47.741 --> 00:39:49.350
and they pay for
only the resources

00:39:49.351 --> 00:39:50.680
consumed by that piece of code.

00:39:50.681 --> 00:39:53.640
So they specify, ''I want
this much RAM for this code,''

00:39:53.641 --> 00:39:55.410
and we bill only

00:39:55.411 --> 00:39:57.150
for the amount of CPU
consumed by the code.

00:39:57.151 --> 00:39:59.445
So, if the code never runs,

00:39:59.446 --> 00:40:01.705
the functions registered
nothing ever happens,

00:40:01.706 --> 00:40:03.390
they don't get charged anything.

00:40:03.391 --> 00:40:07.145
If 1,000 invocations of
that function happen,

00:40:07.146 --> 00:40:08.450
and they each run for a minute,

00:40:08.451 --> 00:40:11.155
then they get billed
1,000 compute minutes.

00:40:11.156 --> 00:40:13.665
So, it's pay-per-use,

00:40:13.666 --> 00:40:16.180
and really great for
bursty type of workloads.

00:40:16.181 --> 00:40:18.090
It's also event-driven
model that is

00:40:18.091 --> 00:40:20.425
now becoming very
popular in the cloud.

00:40:20.426 --> 00:40:22.310
Each of these functions is
triggered by some event.

00:40:22.311 --> 00:40:23.680
For example, some image

00:40:23.681 --> 00:40:25.475
being dropped into
a storage account,

00:40:25.476 --> 00:40:27.540
could kick off a function
that then calls into

00:40:27.541 --> 00:40:30.555
brain wave to go to
inference on that image.

00:40:30.556 --> 00:40:33.850
But, what we've got
with Service Fabric is,

00:40:33.851 --> 00:40:36.150
we're taking that
serverless concept and

00:40:36.151 --> 00:40:37.850
expanding it to general-purpose

00:40:37.851 --> 00:40:39.520
microservices to
general platform,

00:40:39.521 --> 00:40:42.965
not just event-driven
micro functions.

00:40:42.966 --> 00:40:44.860
In this case, we just

00:40:44.861 --> 00:40:46.720
announced the public preview
of this service,

00:40:46.721 --> 00:40:48.790
Service Fabric Mesh, and

00:40:48.791 --> 00:40:52.670
Service Fabric Mesh is
serverless service fabrics.

00:40:52.671 --> 00:40:55.735
You can actually
take a description

00:40:55.736 --> 00:40:58.665
of a microservice application,

00:40:58.666 --> 00:41:01.190
it's even stateful built with

00:41:01.191 --> 00:41:04.325
even abstract programming
models like the actor model,

00:41:04.326 --> 00:41:05.765
and give it to us,

00:41:05.766 --> 00:41:08.265
and we will go and launch it on,

00:41:08.266 --> 00:41:10.365
we'll take care of the
provisioning of virtual machines,

00:41:10.366 --> 00:41:12.370
we'll launch those
containers onto them,

00:41:12.371 --> 00:41:13.620
and then you get paid only for

00:41:13.621 --> 00:41:15.585
the execution of
those containers,

00:41:15.586 --> 00:41:17.959
and never have to worry
about the infrastructure,

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:20.105
only focus on the containers.

00:41:20.106 --> 00:41:23.175
So, this is where
the world is going.

00:41:23.176 --> 00:41:25.850
Is this kind of developers
only worried about their code?

00:41:25.851 --> 00:41:27.580
The platform takes care
of everything else.

00:41:27.581 --> 00:41:29.130
We're at the next stage of

00:41:29.131 --> 00:41:31.770
this and there's still a lot
of work to do in this.

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:34.330
Now, to highlight out one of

00:41:34.331 --> 00:41:36.630
the very productive
collaborations

00:41:36.631 --> 00:41:38.080
we've had with
Microsoft research,

00:41:38.081 --> 00:41:40.335
we've been collaborating
with Microsoft research

00:41:40.336 --> 00:41:42.210
on the problem of
resource allocation in

00:41:42.211 --> 00:41:46.340
our data centers since
cluster one joined Azure,

00:41:46.341 --> 00:41:49.270
about eight years ago,
including Microsoft Azure,

00:41:49.271 --> 00:41:50.780
one of the key researchers
there, in fact,

00:41:50.781 --> 00:41:54.375
we hired him into the Azure
about a year and a half ago.

00:41:54.376 --> 00:41:56.545
He was instrumental in this.

00:41:56.546 --> 00:41:58.170
The collaboration with
Microsoft research

00:41:58.171 --> 00:42:00.880
continues on this project
called Resource Central,

00:42:00.881 --> 00:42:02.120
which is generally looking at,

00:42:02.121 --> 00:42:03.780
how do we make use of resources

00:42:03.781 --> 00:42:05.265
in general more efficient?

00:42:05.266 --> 00:42:07.790
That efficiency comes in
a number of different forms,

00:42:07.791 --> 00:42:10.710
how do we pack
virtual machines more

00:42:10.711 --> 00:42:14.640
tightly and not waste
resources on servers?

00:42:14.641 --> 00:42:18.160
How do we make sure that
an evictable workload doesn't

00:42:18.161 --> 00:42:20.295
interfere with the placement

00:42:20.296 --> 00:42:22.515
of a non-evictable
higher priority workload?

00:42:22.516 --> 00:42:25.770
How do we make sure
that we don't exceed

00:42:25.771 --> 00:42:30.155
power budgets in our data
centers as CPU spins up?

00:42:30.156 --> 00:42:32.330
All of these are
questions that we

00:42:32.331 --> 00:42:35.150
want to get better insights
into out of resource central.

00:42:35.151 --> 00:42:37.870
If you take a look at
the resource centrals,

00:42:37.871 --> 00:42:40.010
heavily dependent on
machine-learning algorithms,

00:42:40.011 --> 00:42:44.130
taking in historical data,
from production,

00:42:44.131 --> 00:42:46.160
training on that data,

00:42:46.161 --> 00:42:49.325
and then generating models
that we then deploy

00:42:49.326 --> 00:42:51.770
as offline services that are

00:42:51.771 --> 00:42:54.180
integrated with
the online resource allocator.

00:42:54.181 --> 00:42:56.185
They're offline in the sense that

00:42:56.186 --> 00:43:00.450
the running system does

00:43:00.451 --> 00:43:03.465
not depend on the availability
of those services.

00:43:03.466 --> 00:43:05.615
These are optimizations.

00:43:05.616 --> 00:43:07.940
One of the principles
in the cloud is,

00:43:07.941 --> 00:43:10.160
you want as few dependencies

00:43:10.161 --> 00:43:12.670
on the runtime operation of
the service as possible,

00:43:12.671 --> 00:43:14.460
because failures always happen.

00:43:14.461 --> 00:43:16.240
If there's non-critical
component,

00:43:16.241 --> 00:43:19.125
you don't want to put
that in the hot path.

00:43:19.126 --> 00:43:22.445
But you can look at the problems
that we're looking at,

00:43:22.446 --> 00:43:24.130
virtual machine scheduling, which

00:43:24.131 --> 00:43:26.180
clusters virtual machines go to?

00:43:26.181 --> 00:43:27.690
I mentioned power
over subscription,

00:43:27.691 --> 00:43:29.370
how we do server maintenance in

00:43:29.371 --> 00:43:31.725
efficient way without
impacting customers,

00:43:31.726 --> 00:43:33.600
or minimally impacting customers,

00:43:33.601 --> 00:43:35.050
and then even giving

00:43:35.051 --> 00:43:37.680
customers right sizing
recommendations based on

00:43:37.681 --> 00:43:40.240
looking at historical patterns
of other virtual machines

00:43:40.241 --> 00:43:41.460
that they might be
running other ones

00:43:41.461 --> 00:43:43.160
that are running
similar workloads,

00:43:43.161 --> 00:43:44.920
their historical usage resources

00:43:44.921 --> 00:43:46.220
by that particular
virtual machine.

00:43:46.221 --> 00:43:47.325
We can say, ''Hey,

00:43:47.326 --> 00:43:48.910
looks like you could easily fit

00:43:48.911 --> 00:43:51.260
into a virtual machine
that's much smaller or,

00:43:51.261 --> 00:43:55.510
you need more resources actually,
because you're [inaudible].''

00:43:55.511 --> 00:43:56.595
Here's just one example

00:43:56.596 --> 00:44:00.710
of one of the projects
that we've had ongoing.

00:44:00.711 --> 00:44:02.715
I say on-going because

00:44:02.716 --> 00:44:04.845
these are never kind
of ending projects.

00:44:04.846 --> 00:44:07.445
There's always room
for more optimization.

00:44:07.446 --> 00:44:10.240
As Donna mentioned,
one percent of optimization

00:44:10.241 --> 00:44:13.140
in the right place
can have huge impact.

00:44:13.141 --> 00:44:15.450
When you're talking about
millions of virtual machines,

00:44:15.451 --> 00:44:17.410
and you're talking about
millions of servers,

00:44:17.411 --> 00:44:19.340
saving one percent of

00:44:19.341 --> 00:44:22.710
your resource can literally
mean millions of dollars.

00:44:22.711 --> 00:44:25.550
So, this resource
allocation algorithm

00:44:25.551 --> 00:44:27.720
that we've developed with MSR,

00:44:27.721 --> 00:44:31.870
assigns each resource dimension,

00:44:31.871 --> 00:44:34.090
assigns it a weight which

00:44:34.091 --> 00:44:37.545
is relative to the scarcity
that resource,

00:44:37.546 --> 00:44:41.040
and then runs this optimization
problem where we try

00:44:41.041 --> 00:44:42.860
to maximize the availability of

00:44:42.861 --> 00:44:44.995
those scarce resources
through our allocations.

00:44:44.996 --> 00:44:48.690
You can see there on the right,
two example allocations.

00:44:48.691 --> 00:44:49.850
Obviously, the one
on the right is a

00:44:49.851 --> 00:44:51.760
better one for
this particular server.

00:44:51.761 --> 00:44:55.790
If we allocate two low
memory machines meaning,

00:44:55.791 --> 00:44:57.860
they consume a lot of course,

00:44:57.861 --> 00:44:59.580
but little memory,

00:44:59.581 --> 00:45:01.630
we waste a bunch of
memory on that server.

00:45:01.631 --> 00:45:04.710
But if we take one of
those low memory machines,

00:45:04.711 --> 00:45:07.020
take another one that's not
consuming as many CPU core,

00:45:07.021 --> 00:45:09.190
so we've got additional
CPU cores there left for

00:45:09.191 --> 00:45:11.000
virtual machine that is

00:45:11.001 --> 00:45:12.220
a high memory virtual machine few

00:45:12.221 --> 00:45:14.090
cores relative to
the amount of RAM,

00:45:14.091 --> 00:45:15.630
we can actually make use of all

00:45:15.631 --> 00:45:16.650
the resources on the server.

00:45:16.651 --> 00:45:18.215
So, that's just a simple look

00:45:18.216 --> 00:45:19.970
at the optimization problem.

00:45:19.971 --> 00:45:22.860
Here's another example
where we take a look at

00:45:22.861 --> 00:45:26.010
historical eviction patterns
for virtual machines,

00:45:26.011 --> 00:45:29.190
and use them to influence
the allocation algorithm.

00:45:29.191 --> 00:45:32.440
So, we've got
two virtual machines a,

00:45:32.441 --> 00:45:33.890
and we know b is
going to becoming.

00:45:33.891 --> 00:45:36.625
B is a full server
virtual machine,

00:45:36.626 --> 00:45:38.800
a is a one-fourth
virtual machine.

00:45:38.801 --> 00:45:40.400
There's two servers
that we've got to pick

00:45:40.401 --> 00:45:42.250
from to allocate this.

00:45:42.251 --> 00:45:44.050
Both can fit this
virtual machine a.

00:45:44.051 --> 00:45:45.900
The question is,
which of these is

00:45:45.901 --> 00:45:49.360
the better fit if
the probability that

00:45:49.361 --> 00:45:52.070
a virtual machine is
going to terminate by

00:45:52.071 --> 00:45:56.135
time t when we need to allocate
virtual machine b is 1.5?

00:45:56.136 --> 00:45:58.950
You can see the
probability if we deploy

00:45:58.951 --> 00:46:02.090
that virtual machine
a on node one, well,

00:46:02.091 --> 00:46:04.785
the probability of it terminating

00:46:04.786 --> 00:46:06.920
those two VMs that
are done on node one

00:46:06.921 --> 00:46:08.945
terminating by time
T is 1.5 squared,

00:46:08.946 --> 00:46:11.985
the probability of one of
those three virtual machines,

00:46:11.986 --> 00:46:14.005
all three terminating
on node two,

00:46:14.006 --> 00:46:16.185
to make room for
that virtual machine b

00:46:16.186 --> 00:46:17.695
is 1.5 to the third,

00:46:17.696 --> 00:46:18.670
and you can see that so

00:46:18.671 --> 00:46:20.650
the probability that
time T that will be

00:46:20.651 --> 00:46:23.985
able to allocate virtual
machine b is six over 16.

00:46:23.986 --> 00:46:26.820
But if we place the
virtual machine on node two,

00:46:26.821 --> 00:46:28.380
all odds get better that virtual

00:46:28.381 --> 00:46:29.965
machine b will find a home.

00:46:29.966 --> 00:46:31.240
So, that's another example of

00:46:31.241 --> 00:46:34.115
the resource
allocation strategies

00:46:34.116 --> 00:46:37.875
that we've developed in
conjunction with MSR.

00:46:37.876 --> 00:46:40.470
You can see we've used a bunch

00:46:40.471 --> 00:46:42.220
of different machine
learning algorithms

00:46:42.221 --> 00:46:45.800
depending on the problem for
CPU utilization modeling,

00:46:45.801 --> 00:46:47.390
random forest, for a lifetime

00:46:47.391 --> 00:46:50.460
the virtual machine
gradient boosting trees.

00:46:50.461 --> 00:46:53.120
The accuracy of these models

00:46:53.121 --> 00:46:54.800
as we develop them
is extremely high.

00:46:54.801 --> 00:46:57.290
High enough of
course, that they are

00:46:57.291 --> 00:46:58.920
having significant impact on

00:46:58.921 --> 00:47:01.280
our efficiency in
the data center.

00:47:04.660 --> 00:47:08.640
So, let me now switch
to Azure Storage.

00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:12.175
Azure Storage,
there's three layers

00:47:12.176 --> 00:47:14.195
in the architecture roughly.

00:47:14.196 --> 00:47:16.360
So, you can see the
front ends there,

00:47:16.361 --> 00:47:17.650
you can see a partition layer

00:47:17.651 --> 00:47:19.465
which partitions
the data and then,

00:47:19.466 --> 00:47:22.230
you can see a distributed file
system layer which handles

00:47:22.231 --> 00:47:25.565
what are called streams
for those partitions.

00:47:25.566 --> 00:47:28.580
That layer is the one that
actually does the replication.

00:47:28.581 --> 00:47:30.795
The others are effectively
the control plane

00:47:30.796 --> 00:47:32.800
and the DFS layers,

00:47:32.801 --> 00:47:34.510
the data plane for
the storage service.

00:47:34.511 --> 00:47:36.915
On top of this, we've
got load balancers,

00:47:36.916 --> 00:47:38.570
and then the storage service

00:47:38.571 --> 00:47:41.210
exposes a number
of different APIs.

00:47:41.211 --> 00:47:43.980
It exposes a simple Blob API

00:47:43.981 --> 00:47:46.200
in both block blob and
page blob forms,

00:47:46.201 --> 00:47:48.590
page blobs something that
we use for virtual machine.

00:47:48.591 --> 00:47:50.415
Hard disks so
page blobs are fixed.

00:47:50.416 --> 00:47:52.165
The page is fixed in size,

00:47:52.166 --> 00:47:55.090
a block blob is arbitrary size.

00:47:55.091 --> 00:47:59.050
Queues which are
simple 5.0 queues.

00:47:59.051 --> 00:48:03.565
File API, which it includes SMB,

00:48:03.566 --> 00:48:07.630
as well as HTFS we recently
announced as well.

00:48:07.631 --> 00:48:10.100
These storage services

00:48:10.101 --> 00:48:12.905
support replication
across regions.

00:48:12.906 --> 00:48:15.910
So, if you deploy something with

00:48:15.911 --> 00:48:21.925
regional geo-replication
or a GRS,

00:48:21.926 --> 00:48:24.075
then what you've got are

00:48:24.076 --> 00:48:26.760
the partition layers
doing replication.

00:48:26.761 --> 00:48:29.270
Now, like many of the pieces of

00:48:29.271 --> 00:48:32.900
Azure that we started
with 10 years ago,

00:48:32.901 --> 00:48:34.570
we've had to
re-architect the system

00:48:34.571 --> 00:48:37.130
as we've gotten to
a larger and larger scale.

00:48:37.131 --> 00:48:40.160
The initial storage architecture

00:48:40.161 --> 00:48:42.440
was based on the storage
stamps or clusters,

00:48:42.441 --> 00:48:45.485
a fixed number of
physical servers,

00:48:45.486 --> 00:48:48.810
and what you've got when
you've got a large datacenter,

00:48:48.811 --> 00:48:51.445
a region with many many stamps is

00:48:51.446 --> 00:48:54.510
inefficiencies and scale limits.

00:48:54.511 --> 00:48:56.900
The scale limits are

00:48:56.901 --> 00:48:59.865
the limits of the servers
in that one stamp,

00:48:59.866 --> 00:49:03.660
or the maximum limits that
we can offer customers for

00:49:03.661 --> 00:49:08.660
throughput and size of
their storage service.

00:49:08.661 --> 00:49:12.125
Then, it's inefficient in
terms of the fragmentation,

00:49:12.126 --> 00:49:13.420
because we've got to have failure

00:49:13.421 --> 00:49:14.760
buffers in each cluster,

00:49:14.761 --> 00:49:16.430
we've got an expansion buffers in

00:49:16.431 --> 00:49:18.540
this cluster and we can't

00:49:18.541 --> 00:49:20.670
amortize those buffers across

00:49:20.671 --> 00:49:23.655
all of that servers in
the datacenter region.

00:49:23.656 --> 00:49:26.170
So, we started re-architecting

00:49:26.171 --> 00:49:30.465
the storage system about
three years ago actually,

00:49:30.466 --> 00:49:33.505
and we are putting this
into production now.

00:49:33.506 --> 00:49:37.855
This is called Gen2
Azure Data Lake Storage.

00:49:37.856 --> 00:49:40.830
We call it internally
limitless storage,

00:49:40.831 --> 00:49:44.250
because if you take a look
at some of the scale limits,

00:49:44.251 --> 00:49:45.920
so a storage account

00:49:45.921 --> 00:49:48.730
limit and we're going to see
these limits even go up,

00:49:48.731 --> 00:49:50.580
50 gigabytes per
second as opposed to

00:49:50.581 --> 00:49:55.165
the 60 megabytes per second
that we're used to have.

00:49:55.166 --> 00:49:57.690
The throughput spikes are

00:49:57.691 --> 00:50:00.640
instantaneous and there's
no warm-up for these.

00:50:00.641 --> 00:50:04.580
The scale, our target is

00:50:04.581 --> 00:50:07.130
that one storage blob could

00:50:07.131 --> 00:50:10.510
consume every single byte of
storage in an entire region.

00:50:10.511 --> 00:50:12.970
That's the kind of
scale limits we're going to,

00:50:12.971 --> 00:50:15.610
so hence the name limitless.

00:50:15.611 --> 00:50:18.030
You can see that
the throughput now that

00:50:18.031 --> 00:50:20.160
we can drive off of being able to

00:50:20.161 --> 00:50:22.500
take advantages a much scale-out

00:50:22.501 --> 00:50:26.900
storage servers as possible
in this architecture.

00:50:26.901 --> 00:50:30.250
We can go from uploading
a five terabyte blob

00:50:31.650 --> 00:50:35.605
in 14 minutes that
previously took,

00:50:35.606 --> 00:50:36.915
you can see 12 hours.

00:50:36.916 --> 00:50:38.420
So, massive improvement in

00:50:38.421 --> 00:50:42.290
the throughput that we get
with this new architecture.

00:50:42.291 --> 00:50:46.575
But we're also looking with
MSR at the future of storage.

00:50:46.576 --> 00:50:49.915
We've got hard disks,
we've got SSDs,

00:50:49.916 --> 00:50:51.460
we also have tape now,

00:50:51.461 --> 00:50:53.750
and we've got also
something called Pelican,

00:50:53.751 --> 00:50:55.060
a project where we've got

00:50:55.061 --> 00:50:59.395
hard disks in basically a
hard disk appliance form,

00:50:59.396 --> 00:51:01.340
a rack of hard disks
where only some of

00:51:01.341 --> 00:51:03.620
the hard disk can be
spin up at a time as

00:51:03.621 --> 00:51:05.590
a low-cost archival
storage solution with

00:51:05.591 --> 00:51:09.735
higher latency than
a single hard disk solution.

00:51:09.736 --> 00:51:11.760
But we want to do better.

00:51:11.761 --> 00:51:13.830
It's all about
maximizing efficiency,

00:51:13.831 --> 00:51:16.280
especially for archival
storage which can be

00:51:16.281 --> 00:51:17.470
around for long periods of

00:51:17.471 --> 00:51:19.200
time without ever being accessed,

00:51:19.201 --> 00:51:21.170
you want that to be
as cheap as possible.

00:51:21.171 --> 00:51:24.285
The world is now storing
massive amounts of data.

00:51:24.286 --> 00:51:26.920
So, we've got two projects
going on with MSR.

00:51:26.921 --> 00:51:28.705
One, it's called Silica

00:51:28.706 --> 00:51:30.880
and it's looking at glass
and I'll explain that one.

00:51:30.881 --> 00:51:32.610
Another one's called Plex and

00:51:32.611 --> 00:51:34.805
it's looking at use
of DNA to store data.

00:51:34.806 --> 00:51:37.525
I don't have time to talk
about Plex this morning,

00:51:37.526 --> 00:51:39.810
but that's another
a very exciting project

00:51:39.811 --> 00:51:41.750
that offers the promise of

00:51:41.751 --> 00:51:43.650
storing a full zettabyte of data

00:51:43.651 --> 00:51:46.135
in a single rack in a datacenter.

00:51:46.136 --> 00:51:48.510
Just to put that
into perspective,

00:51:48.511 --> 00:51:50.230
the estimate is
that there's around

00:51:50.231 --> 00:51:52.935
20 zettabytes of data
on the planet today.

00:51:52.936 --> 00:51:54.840
So, 20 racks and you could store

00:51:54.841 --> 00:51:58.415
all the data in
the world on Plex.

00:51:58.416 --> 00:52:00.470
But going to glass, let me
talk a little bit about,

00:52:00.471 --> 00:52:02.550
this is a project with
the University of Southampton

00:52:02.551 --> 00:52:06.600
collaboration with MSR Cambridge
there and us in Azure.

00:52:06.601 --> 00:52:10.915
The idea here is to store
data in glass, obviously.

00:52:10.916 --> 00:52:14.525
Now, that might seem
straightforward,

00:52:14.526 --> 00:52:16.130
just etch the data
into the glass.

00:52:16.131 --> 00:52:18.060
The problem is up to now,

00:52:18.061 --> 00:52:19.690
there's been no way
to efficiently

00:52:19.691 --> 00:52:21.980
store data at high
density in glass.

00:52:21.981 --> 00:52:25.260
Because whenever somebody
tries to etch it with lasers,

00:52:25.261 --> 00:52:27.255
they end up with defects.

00:52:27.256 --> 00:52:31.420
The defects caused by
material deformations because

00:52:31.421 --> 00:52:33.070
the laser pulses are

00:52:33.071 --> 00:52:37.185
damaging the atomic
structure of the glass.

00:52:37.186 --> 00:52:41.000
So, you might write something
but you can't read it back.

00:52:41.001 --> 00:52:43.130
What the University of Southampton
and Microsoft research

00:52:43.131 --> 00:52:44.540
had been working on are

00:52:44.541 --> 00:52:47.400
using high-speed lasers that

00:52:47.401 --> 00:52:50.205
operate at the femtosecond range,

00:52:50.206 --> 00:52:54.490
which is the same pulse
duration that you might

00:52:54.491 --> 00:52:59.640
have cataract surgery
with or lens surgery.

00:52:59.641 --> 00:53:01.345
How long is a femtosecond?

00:53:01.346 --> 00:53:04.340
It's really short, I
had to look it up.

00:53:06.220 --> 00:53:08.760
With a femtosecond laser,

00:53:08.761 --> 00:53:10.720
we can pulse and

00:53:10.721 --> 00:53:13.125
generate what are called
voxels in the glass,

00:53:13.126 --> 00:53:14.870
which are three-dimensional
structures.

00:53:14.871 --> 00:53:17.800
You can see here that
it have some depth.

00:53:17.801 --> 00:53:20.885
By targeting laser at
different depths in the glass,

00:53:20.886 --> 00:53:22.300
we can create multiple layers of

00:53:22.301 --> 00:53:24.310
this in one piece of glass.

00:53:24.311 --> 00:53:26.490
I was given a sample yesterday by

00:53:26.491 --> 00:53:28.995
the project Silica team
that has 76 layers in it.

00:53:28.996 --> 00:53:30.740
So, we've gotten now where

00:53:30.741 --> 00:53:32.615
we can store very
efficiently vertically,

00:53:32.616 --> 00:53:35.315
as well as horizontally

00:53:35.316 --> 00:53:39.690
with five-micron separation
between these voxels.

00:53:40.840 --> 00:53:44.200
Here's a look at
the reader for this.

00:53:44.201 --> 00:53:47.120
The interesting thing about
glass is once you write it,

00:53:47.121 --> 00:53:48.715
you never have to refresh it,

00:53:48.716 --> 00:53:51.540
it lasts for literally millions
of years longer,

00:53:51.541 --> 00:53:53.040
or billions of years, really.

00:53:53.041 --> 00:53:57.460
So, outlasting
even probably the earth.

00:53:57.770 --> 00:54:00.695
You don't need
a specialized reader,

00:54:00.696 --> 00:54:01.910
you don't need
a specialized reading

00:54:01.911 --> 00:54:02.945
technology to read it;

00:54:02.946 --> 00:54:04.305
all you need is light.

00:54:04.306 --> 00:54:06.125
So, flashing light on this

00:54:06.126 --> 00:54:07.550
and looking at the reflections of

00:54:07.551 --> 00:54:09.220
the light or how that light

00:54:09.221 --> 00:54:11.255
is diffracting off those voxels,

00:54:11.256 --> 00:54:13.355
you can read the data off of it.

00:54:13.356 --> 00:54:17.200
So, this is our early look
at a read head.

00:54:17.201 --> 00:54:20.900
You can see that this one
is going through 20 layers,

00:54:20.901 --> 00:54:23.320
showing a simple image
of those 20 layers,

00:54:23.321 --> 00:54:27.550
so each of those depths.

00:54:27.551 --> 00:54:30.880
Then, here's a look at
how we decode the data.

00:54:30.881 --> 00:54:32.730
We take multiple images at

00:54:32.731 --> 00:54:35.875
a layer using
different conditions,

00:54:35.876 --> 00:54:37.580
and then we look
at the retardance

00:54:37.581 --> 00:54:40.255
and orientation and then

00:54:40.256 --> 00:54:41.560
map that to orientation and

00:54:41.561 --> 00:54:44.590
retardance where we can encode
data in those two values.

00:54:44.591 --> 00:54:48.070
You can see here an example
of a three bit per

00:54:48.071 --> 00:54:52.990
voxel example of
encoding data on that glass.

00:54:53.250 --> 00:54:56.725
What this promises in
terms of density too,

00:54:56.726 --> 00:54:58.680
it's story about 50 terabytes of

00:54:58.681 --> 00:55:01.365
data into centimeter square.

00:55:01.366 --> 00:55:03.550
So, this is orders
of magnitude more

00:55:03.551 --> 00:55:04.890
dense than the kinds

00:55:04.891 --> 00:55:07.250
of archival data that
we have had now.

00:55:07.251 --> 00:55:11.345
This would be a huge breakthrough
for archival storage.

00:55:11.346 --> 00:55:14.670
It's one of those
long-term projects we got.

00:55:14.671 --> 00:55:16.110
MSR it's multiple years,

00:55:16.111 --> 00:55:17.860
significant amount of
investment, of course,

00:55:17.861 --> 00:55:20.465
and material technology and

00:55:20.466 --> 00:55:22.590
equipment that needs to be taken

00:55:22.591 --> 00:55:24.500
from a lab prototype and then

00:55:24.501 --> 00:55:27.960
made datacenter production ready.

00:55:27.961 --> 00:55:30.495
And we're all doing
it in collaboration.

00:55:30.496 --> 00:55:32.450
So, that brings me to
the conclusion of the talk.

00:55:32.451 --> 00:55:34.430
Here, I give you a brief overview

00:55:34.431 --> 00:55:36.610
of a high-level
Azure Architecture,

00:55:36.611 --> 00:55:40.130
look at some of
the interesting MSR projects.

00:55:40.131 --> 00:55:41.740
The bottom line is

00:55:41.741 --> 00:55:44.410
that when it comes to research
in the Cloud besides,

00:55:44.411 --> 00:55:46.840
of course, innovative
programming models,

00:55:46.841 --> 00:55:50.749
innovative typologies,
communications architectures;

00:55:50.750 --> 00:55:52.870
much of the research
that we focus on is

00:55:52.871 --> 00:55:55.710
driving up efficiency and
scale and performance.

00:55:55.711 --> 00:55:58.655
You can look at the
projects that I showed you,

00:55:58.656 --> 00:56:02.010
they're all aimed at efficiency
scale and performance.

00:56:02.011 --> 00:56:04.135
They're just some of the projects

00:56:04.136 --> 00:56:05.645
that we've got going with MSR.

00:56:05.646 --> 00:56:08.020
I mentioned Plex, we've

00:56:08.021 --> 00:56:10.625
got another one optical
networking called Sirius,

00:56:10.626 --> 00:56:12.390
we've got another one that

00:56:12.391 --> 00:56:13.860
you're going to hear
about here if you go to

00:56:13.861 --> 00:56:16.945
Albert Greenberg's talk
on Crystal Net which is

00:56:16.946 --> 00:56:18.970
simulating or wide area

00:56:18.971 --> 00:56:20.080
networks so that we can make

00:56:20.081 --> 00:56:21.880
configuration changes safely.

00:56:21.881 --> 00:56:23.680
The list goes on and on.

00:56:23.681 --> 00:56:26.550
Like I said, it's a competitive
advantage of us to have

00:56:26.551 --> 00:56:29.825
MSR here to go out and
explore these kinds of areas.

00:56:29.826 --> 00:56:31.690
In some cases, ones that

00:56:31.691 --> 00:56:33.480
seem far-fetched to us
when we initially look

00:56:33.481 --> 00:56:35.990
at them and offer

00:56:35.991 --> 00:56:38.030
a bunch of research problems
like Project Natick,

00:56:38.031 --> 00:56:40.005
that if they are realized,

00:56:40.006 --> 00:56:41.460
it could really
transform our business.

00:56:41.461 --> 00:56:44.610
So, with that, I
want to wrap it up.

00:56:44.611 --> 00:56:47.880
Again, I want to
thank Eric and Donald

00:56:47.881 --> 00:56:49.950
and Dan for inviting me to open

00:56:49.951 --> 00:56:52.185
up this faculty summit with you.

00:56:52.186 --> 00:56:55.095
I hope you have got
a fantastic few days here,

00:56:55.096 --> 00:56:57.080
a bunch of interesting talks
you've got ahead of

00:56:57.081 --> 00:57:00.120
you and thank you very much.

